\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rises on coronavirus unease, Aussie and Kiwi hurt by rate outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2020\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Graphic: World \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in 2020 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2RBWI5E"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2RBWI5E}}}\par \par \uc2 * Dollar holds strong gains against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Response to second wave of virus rattles investors\par \par \uc2 * U.S. political uncertainty to limit dollar's upside\par \par \uc2 * Aussie and kiwi fall on interest rate outlook\par \par \uc2 By Stanley White\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 23 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar rose against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, supported by positive U.S. economic data and concerns about a second wave of coronavirus infections in Europe and Britain.\par \par \uc2 Both the Australian and New Zealand dollars were undermined by a dovish slant on policy by their respective central banks.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie hit a six-week low on growing expectations the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) may cut interest rates next month.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi dollar edged lower in choppy trade after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept monetary policy unchanged but hinted at further easing to help the economy.\par \par \uc2 The greenback is likely to continue to grind higher in the short term as the coronavirus rattles sentiment in Europe, but uncertainty about this year's U.S. presidential election means the dollar could be prone to more volatile swings.\par \par \uc2 "Some people are betting for more dollar strength against the euro, which looks overvalued," said Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "The picture in Europe has completely changed, because the economic recovery is stalling and there is a second wave of the virus, but I'm also worried about U.S. politics."\par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up to $1.1675 per euro on Wednesday, which is the highest since July 27.\par \par \uc2 The pound bought $1.2721, near the lowest since late July, after British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  unveiled on Tuesday new restrictions on business activity to tackle a second wave of the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to 0.9218 Swiss franc, adding to a 0.6% gain from Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to 105.98 yen.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the greenback was bolstered by data showing U.S. home sales surged to their highest level in nearly 14 years in August, but comments from a prominent \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  official sent mixed signals.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy risks a longer, slower recovery and "recessionary dynamics" if Congress fails to pass an additional fiscal stimulus package, Chicago Federal Reserve President \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 It is possible for the Fed to raise interest rates before inflation starts to average 2%, Evans also said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which pits the dollar against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rose to 94.197 on Wednesday, the highest in two months.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment for the euro has slowly weakened as investors grow increasingly worried about surging coronavirus infections in countries like France and Spain, raising the risk of fresh lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 Many euro zone countries have reintroduced travel restrictions, forcing airlines to scale back passenger services after a relatively quick run up over the summer.\par \par \uc2 Traders in the pound and the euro are also worried that Britain and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  will fail to agree a free trade deal, which would cause additional economic strain.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar fell to a six-week low of $0.7116 after economists at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  changed their view and said they expect the RBA to lower interest rates to 0.10% from 0.25% at a meeting on Oct. 6.\par \par \uc2 The tone for the Aussie was also weak after a senior central banker on Tuesday flagged the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and negative interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar lost 0.33% to $0.6612 after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  held its policy rate at 0.25%, but warned of job losses and business closures, which reinforced expectations it would move to negative interest rates in coming months. (Reporting by Stanley White; Editing by Christopher Cushing and Ana Nicolaci da Costa) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-23T06:01:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200923eg9n00id6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
