\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares set for rough ride on virus fears, eyes on China\b0\par\par\uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2020\par 01:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds MSCI ex-Japan and Nikkei, updates levels throughout)\par \par \uc2 * Asian stock markets : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2zpUAr4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2zpUAr4 }}}\par \par \uc2 * Australian, NZ shares fall; oil slides, gold firm\par \par \uc2 * Coronavirus death toll in China rises to 350\par \par \uc2 * China cbank to inject $174 bln liquidity on Monday\par \par \uc2 * Economists lower growth forecasts for Chinese economy\par \par \uc2 By Swati Pandey\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Asian markets are set for another bumpy ride on Monday on fears about the hit to world growth from the rapidly spreading coronavirus, with all eyes on China where trading resumes following the Lunar New Year break.\par \par \uc2 A total of 361 people have died in China from the new virus with the first death out of the mainland reported on Sunday in the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 Looking to head off any panic, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to inject 1.2 trillion yuan ($173.8 billion) of liquidity into the markets via reverse repo \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Beijing also said it would help firms that produce vital goods resume work as soon as possible, state broadcaster CCTV reported.\par \par \uc2 Despite the measures, MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was 0.5% lower, on track for an eighth straight day of losses.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei stumbled 1.5% and South Korea's KOSPI index was off 1.4%. Australia's benchmark index was down 0.7%, while \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  shares fell 1.8%.\par \par \uc2 "These initial \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aim to boost confidence, but they are unlikely to be sufficient to curtail a sharp downturn in Q1," Citi economists said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "As most employees won't return to work until Feb. 9, the output losses are likely to be larger than expected, and incoming economic activity data will continue to prompt the authorities to take more actions in order to reduce the adverse impact of the Wuhan coronavirus on the economy."\par \par \uc2 Chris Weston, a Sydney-based strategist at broker Pepperston, said "the big unknown" was how China's financial markets respond to the show of force from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The fact the \uc2 China Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC) \uc2 has detailed they see the impact of the coronavirus as 'short-lived' is designed to instil confidence," Weston said. "Whether the market feeds off this optimism is another thing given the spread of the virus is still in its exponential stage."\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Dow fell 2.1%, the S&P 500 declined 1.8% and the Nasdaq Composite dropped 1.6% as economists tempered their outlook for the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of a pause in the selloff elsewhere, E-Mini futures for the S&P500 added 0.2% on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect travel curbs and supply chain disruptions to crimp Chinese growth, with a potential domino effect on other economies.\par \par \uc2 Citi revised its full-year forecast for China's GDP growth to 5.5% in 2020 from 5.8%. It also cut first-quarter growth expectations to 4.8%, compared with 6% in the fourth quarter of 2019.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  shaved its forecast for global growth by 0.3 percentage points for this quarter.\par \par \uc2 Data out of the United States and Europe on Friday also pointed to economic weakness, while a mixed batch of corporate earnings added to the gloom.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the safe-haven Japanese yen held near a 3-1/2 week high against the dollar at 108.41 after adding about 1.5% in the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The risk sensitive Australian dollar, which is often traded as a liquid proxy for the Chinese yuan, tumbled 2% last week to hit a four-month trough of $0.6683. It was last up 0.1% at 0.6694.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the greenback against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was a shade higher at 97.439.\par \par \uc2 Gold, which posted its best month in five in January, eased to $1,588.72, while yields on U.S. debt lingered near five-month lows as the United States, Japan and other countries tightened travel curbs to China.\par \par \uc2 Oil futures skidded on concerns the coronavirus outbreak would hit China's oil demand. Brent crude slid $1.06 to $55.56 a barrel, the lowest since January 2019. U.S. crude slipped 84 cents to $50.72.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Sam Holmes and Richard Pullin) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-2-3T01:53:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200203eg230048t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
