\par\par \b \uc2 USD/SGD Edges Higher, Unlikely to Show Volatility Near-Term -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2019\par 09:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0759 GMT - USD/SGD edges higher against the backdrop of U.S.-China trade war. The pair is unlikely to show volatile movement as investors wait for more signals regarding the U.S.-China trade talks, Maybank says, adding SGD could strengthen in the near-term due to Singapore's status as a safe haven in the region. The investment bank puts support at 1.3760 with resistance at 1.3850. USD/SGD was last at 1.3832, from 1.3816 in late New York trade on Tuesday. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT - German industrial production fell more than expected in June, pointing to a contraction of Europe's largest economy in the second quarter, ING's chief economist for Germany, \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  , says. "Looking ahead, despite yesterday's encouraging industrial orders data, the combination of high inventories and few orders at hand does not bode well for industrial production in the months ahead," he says. Trade disputes, Brexit and massive structural changes in the automotive sector further dampen the outlook, he notes. "Against this background, recent tentative signs that the domestic economy's resilience is crumbling are concerning," he adds. (max.bernhard@dowjones.com; @mxbernhard)\par \par \uc2 0734 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose about eight billion francs ($8.2 billion) in July to 767.9 billion francs. The franc was largely stable last month against the dollar and euro but given the massive size of the \uc2 SNB\uc2 's reserves, even a few tenths of a percentage point can influence the value of its reserves. In addition, weekly \uc2 SNB\uc2  sight deposits data suggest the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the franc toward the end of July, which also would have added to their stockpile of foreign reserves. EUR/CHF down 0.1% at 1.0929 francs.(brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT - The safe-haven yen and Swiss franc rise again, with the USD/JPY down 0.2% at 106.25 and the EUR/CHF down 0.1% at 1.0931, and are expected to remain in favor as concerns grow about global trade tensions and economic weakness. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  's unexpectedly large 50-basis-point rate cut sparked talk of a "race to the bottom" in interest rates, \uc2 Neil Wilson\uc2  , analyst at Markets.com, says. "There's going to be no stopping central banks rushing to cut rates further. This should exacerbate the kind of haven flows we've seen lately," he says. This should mean further yen and franc appreciation, albeit limited by \uc2 Swiss National Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he says. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0526 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's NZX-50 index rallied into the close after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the official cash rate by more than expected. The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  lowered the benchmark rate by 50 basis points to 1.0% on a worsening global outlook, defying market estimates for a reduction of 25 bp. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecasts suggest another cut is likely, and analysts predict it could come anytime between November and February. The \uc2 NZX\uc2  closed 1.9% higher at 10786.26, recouping all of Tuesday's losses and then some. \uc2 Mercury NZ\uc2  gained 5.6% to NZ$4.94, \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  advanced 3.1% to NZ$7.92 and Chorus rose 4.7% to NZ$5.62. \uc2 Pushpay\uc2  was an outlier, down 1.2% at NZ$3.21, while Vista dropped 2.7% to NZ$5.50. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT - Pricing for the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  to cut in September jumped from a 45% to a 70% chance after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  cut by 50 bps at its policy meeting. Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  , says that whether you agree with a September \uc2 RBA\uc2  easing or not, the market is likely to lean in that direction near term. This was the catalyst for the Aussie dollar to finally break through its January intraday lows and trade below US$0.6700 for the first time since the aftermath of the global financial crisis in March 2009, Callow adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT - A tentative AUD recovery has been punctured in spectacular fashion by the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2 's "shock and Orr" 50 basis point rate cut, says Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  . The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  explained its aggressive move by pointing to softer global growth, the U.S.-China trade war and likely weak imported inflation, all of which applies to the \uc2 RBA\uc2  too, he adds. Moreover, Governor Adrian Orr took this bold step despite New Zealand's unemployment slipping below 4%. If the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  is willing to "go big" with sub-4% unemployment, Callow asked, how long can the \uc2 RBA\uc2  hold off on further easing? AUD/USD has fallen to 0.6697 from 0.6780. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0501 GMT - There was clearly an element of "preemptiveness" in the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2 's decision to cut interest rates by 50 basis points to 1%, says \uc2 Sally Auld\uc2  , chief economist at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved to get in front of its global counterparts by applying increased downward pressure on the New Zealand dollar amid rising uncertainty in the global economy, which now involves direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation in China and threats from the White House to perhaps do the same. NZD/USD has fallen from 0.6556 down to 0.6398 currently in Asia(james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT - \uc2 CLP Holdings\uc2  ' Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which weighed on the company's performance in 1H, are likely to deliver positive results in coming quarters, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says, while upgrading the stock to buy from neutral and raising its target price to HK$93.00 from HK$91.50. CLP's 2H operating profits from Australia are likely to come in at HK$1.05 billion, a 27% increase from 1H, the investment bank says. Although the group's 2019 net profit is expected to fall on year, \uc2 Citi\uc2  estimates its earnings will be higher in 2020 and 2021, making higher dividends possible. CLP shares are up 0.7% at HK$82.55. (yifan.wang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT - The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  has cut rates dramatically by 50 basis points, but it may not be done easing policy, says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sounded more concerned about trade tensions, saying that heightened global uncertainty was reducing investment and suppressing trading-partner growth. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  agreed with the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  that the balance of risks to achieving its consumer price inflation and maximum sustainable employment objectives was tilted to the downside. It expects the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  to cut rates to 0.75% from 1.0% in February. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT - The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  surprises with a 50-basis-point cut in the official cash rate to 1.0%, and \uc2 ASB\uc2  notes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecast now has a low of 0.9%, so an easing bias remains in place. \uc2 ASB\uc2  expects the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  to cut the rate again by 25 basis points in November, although the timing will be heavily influenced by global risks, which are fluid at present. "Weak business confidence and growing global concerns were flagged as added risks to the NZ economic outlook," \uc2 ASB\uc2  says. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT - The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  has taken emergency action to support the country's small agriculture-rich economy from the U.S.-China trade war and slowing world growth, cutting interest rates by 50 basis points to 1.0%, surprising the market which had largely expected a 25-basis-point cut. The cut has weakened the New Zealand dollar. Global economic activity continues to weaken, easing demand for New Zealand's goods and services, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says. "Central banks are easing monetary policy to support their economies," it notes. NZD/USD has fallen from 0.6555 down to 0.6425 after the surprise move. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 07, 2019 03:59 ET (07:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190807ef87000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
