\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-New government in New Zealand could spell changes for pioneering  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2017\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Labour, NZ First want to add employment to RBNZ mandate\par \par \uc2 * That would mark shift for pioneer of inflation-targeting\par \par \uc2 * NZ First also wants greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Ana Nicolaci da Costa and Charlotte Greenfield\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Oct 12 (Reuters) - The formation of a centre-left government in New Zealand after an inconclusive election last month would likely spell big changes for its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the pioneer of the inflation-targeting regime adopted across the world.\par \par \uc2 The Labour-Green bloc has an even chance with the ruling National Party to form a government, if it can agree to a deal with the nationalist New Zealand First Party after talks this week.\par \par \uc2 For 28 years, \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had the single aim of keeping inflation between a set range. But Labour wants to add employment to the bank's mandate, a goal shared by NZ First which also wants to broaden the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 's (RBNZ) focus to include greater management of the local dollar's value against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's a huge change. We've had over 25 years of an extraordinarily successful monetary policy that has been copied around the world," said Arthur Grimes, RBNZ's chief economist in the early 1990s and Board Chair between 2003 and 2013. Any change without careful consideration and analysis would be "extraordinary", he added.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand was first to formally grant its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence and first, in 1989, to introduce an official inflation target after grappling with annual price rises as high as 18 percent during the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Though its economy is only the world's 53rd largest, New Zealand has earned a reputation for its economic experimentation and free market approach over the past three decades.\par \par \uc2 Its inflation targeting soon became the global economic orthodoxy.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think people treated it terribly seriously (at first)," said Ted Truman, a former U.S. Federal Reserve official, who spent a month in New Zealand 16 years ago researching inflation targeting.\par \par \uc2 "Five or six years later it was much more respected and people flocked to New Zealand to find out how they were doing it... which is why I was there."\par \par \uc2 Don Brash, who was governor when the inflation target was introduced, said the idea came from a TV interview with Roger Douglas, a Labour finance minister in the 1980s. Douglas was asked if he was happy with a drop in inflation to below 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 "He said: 'No, I'm looking at price stability like zero to 1 percent' and that was I think the first formulation of the target in numerical terms," Brash told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The initial 0-2 percent target was widened in 1996 and then lifted in 2002 to its current 1-3 percent.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's per capita income dropped during the radical reforms of the 1980s but has since stabilised. Its employment rate has consistently out-performed other developed countries for the past decade and inflation has remained in check.\par \par \uc2 DUAL-MANDATE CLUB\par \par \uc2 Labour has said it is committed to the inflation target but wants to add the goal of full employment, bringing it in line with the likes of Australia and the United States.\par \par \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) Governor \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  is a fan of his broader mandate which comprises stable inflation, full employment and "economic prosperity".\par \par \uc2 "It's meant that we've been able to take our time in getting inflation back (to target)," he said recently. "We're prepared to be patient."\par \par \uc2 Grimes, however, argues that history proves monetary policy cannot have a sustained impact on employment.\par \par \uc2 "It would be like having someone who is running for health minister argue for a cancer drug to be used for heart issues," said Grimes, who was in close contact with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials from Britain as they moved to inflation targeting.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the proposed change could lead to a higher bar for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates as the RBNZ balances the need to keep inflation in check with that of securing employment growth - a trade off other central banks with dual mandates have had to grapple with.\par \par \uc2 "I had a conversation with \uc2 Alan Greenspan\uc2  once and he expressed envy for the fact that we had a single mandate," Brash said. "He understands full well if the two are in conflict at some point, which one do you give priority to?"\par \par \uc2 Greenspan did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 POLICY SINGAPORE-STYLE\par \par \uc2 NZ First also favours greater \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with leader \uc2 Winston Peters\uc2  even touting a Singapore-style system where a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  target path replaces official interest rates and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings.\par \par \uc2 Paul Dales\uc2 , chief Australia and \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Capital Economics in Sydney said this would represent "a complete shake-up" of New Zealand's monetary policy and was unlikely to materialize.\par \par \uc2 He said greater \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also unlikely given the RBNZ is bound by its "traffic light" system which guides any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that any changes "would be tantamount to political interference in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 Instead, Labour could agree to make a mention of the exchange rate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy target agreement, along with price stability and full employment.\par \par \uc2 "If they put something like that in, it would be a rhetorical win for Winston ... it wouldn't necessarily change very much in substance at all," said Michael Reddell, a former RBNZ official.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ana Nicolaci da Costa and Charlotte Greenfield; Additional reporting by Wayne Cole in Sydney; Editing by Lincoln Feast) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-10-12T06:01:38.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171012edac007yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
