\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-NZ First touts progress in talks to form govt, but decision delayed\b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2017\par 04:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * NZ First leader says huge progress made in negotiations\par \par \uc2 * Decision announcement delayed until after Thursday\par \par \uc2 * NZ dollar rises on bargain hunting (Adds NZ First leader quote, market/\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaction)\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Oct 11 (Reuters) - The leader of the small nationalist party that will decide New Zealand's next government said on Wednesday that "huge" progress had been made in coalition talks, but he has dropped a self-imposed deadline of Oct. 12 to announce the new government.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Bill English's ruling National Party won 56 seats at the Sept. 23 election, while a Labour-Green bloc have 54 seats, leaving both needing New Zealand First's nine seats to meet the 61 seats needed for a majority in parliament.\par \par \uc2 "We're making huge progress finding what we agree on, what we don't agree on, what we can still negotiate on, where we might take things into the future by co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," NZ First leader \uc2 Winston Peters\uc2  said after four days of talks.\par \par \uc2 Peters told local media on Tuesday evening that he would not be ready to announce his choice by Thursday - a self-imposed deadline.\par \par \uc2 Peters has held the balance of power now three times and has previously served in both National and Labour governments.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar has fallen around 3.7 percent since the election against the U.S. dollar. The political uncertainty this week took the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest levels since late May at $0.7052.\par \par \uc2 The Kiwi has also been under pressure over concerns \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  First would see the new government adopt more economically \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. The party favours restrictions on foreign ownership and more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Peters fuelled those concerns by telling reporters that exporters would welcome the recent fall in the local dollar, adding: "If you're an export-dependent nation, why would you persist with an inflated dollar?"\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Kiwi recovered ground, climbing to $0.7095, its highest since before the final election tally was released on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Investors were taking advantage of the lull before Peters announces his decision to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while it was cheap and get out of short positions, according to brokers.\par \par \uc2 "It's still anyone's guess which way the penny's going to drop with NZ First and people that have been short on the Kiwi will suddenly be thinking: 'I may as well take some risk off the table here'," said Stuart Ive, Wellington-based dealer at OM Financial. (Reporting by Charlotte Greenfield and Ana Nicolaci da Costa; Editing by Gareth Jones) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-10-11T03:02:24.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171011edab00239\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
