\par\par \b \uc2 New Zealand parties kick off fourth day of talks after key decision delay\b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2017\par 22:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Oct 11 (Reuters) - A small New Zealand party that holds the balance of power after an inconclusive election will hold a fourth day of talks aimed at helping to form a government on Wednesday, one day after it said it had delayed a decision on which party it would back.\par \par \uc2 Winston Peters\uc2 , the leader of the nationalist New Zealand First party, told local media on Tuesday evening that he would not be ready to announce his choice by Thursday - a self-imposed deadline.\par \par \uc2 With neither the ruling National Party nor the opposition Labour Party gaining enough seats to form a government at the Sept. 23 vote, both are reliant on New Zealand First, which has in the past worked under governments from both sides.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar has fallen around 3.7 percent since the election against the U.S. dollar. The political uncertainty this week took the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest levels since late May at $0.7052.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a low of $0.7056 overnight and was last up 0.11 percent at $0.7072.\par \par \uc2 "Coalition talks may be completed by Thursday, but the outcome is now not expected until sometime after that," BNZ bank said in a research note. "So the uncertainty of government formation will hang over the NZD for a while yet."\par \par \uc2 That uncertainty has been compounded by concerns that a government including the nationalist \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  First would lead to more economically \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 NZ First has said it favours more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Peters fuelled those concerns by telling reporters that exporters would welcome the recent fall in the local dollar, adding: "If you're an export-dependent nation, why would you persist with an inflated dollar?" (Reporting by Ana Nicolaci da Costa; Editing by Gareth Jones) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-10-10T21:54:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171010edaa00ufq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
