\par\par \b \uc2 New Zealand forestry a first test in nationalist party's protectionist agenda\b0\par\par\uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2017\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON (Reuters) - Some New Zealand forest owners are suspending replanting of trees and re-thinking investments as the country's third-largest export earner finds itself in the sights of maverick politician \uc2 Winston Peters\uc2 ' protectionist agenda.\par \par \uc2 The election king-maker last week said he would prioritize a restructure of the forest industry in closely-watched coalition talks with major parties after last month's inconclusive election result.\par \par \uc2 The plan includes a possible quota system which would force growers to favor local mills over a higher-paying export market. Exports of forest products totaled around NZ$4.75 billion ($3.36 billion) in 2015, with China the top destination.\par \par \uc2 That has put the Pacific nation's lucrative forestry sector at the heart of concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  First in power will spell greater government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's small, outward facing economy.\par \par \uc2 "Every time the government has done something like that we get what you call a hole in the supply of timber and we're going into a hole now, an undersupply," said Joe Carr, the owner of a privately-held logging business managing 500 hectares of forest in the far north of the country.\par \par \uc2 Carr is holding off replanting 60 hectares of his land due to the possible restrictions and said on average growers were paid a third less to sell logs locally than to export them. He said his son was re-thinking whether his family should stick to forestry after 44 years in the business.\par \par \uc2 FREE-MARKETEER\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's plantation forests, like much of the country's industry, were opened up to private investment in the early 1990s after sweeping economic reforms the previous decade. Peters' comments signal a stark change from New Zealand's role as a global test-bed for free market reforms since the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand First Party is also pushing for significant curbs on migration, restrictions on foreign ownership of land and favors more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Peters, a consummate pragmatist, is considered unlikely to insist on his more radical ideas. He has served in governments with both the Labour and National parties in the past without major economic overhaul.\par \par \uc2 But the 72-year old is also looking to cement his legacy in what is likely to be his last major hold on power after 40 years in politics.\par \par \uc2 "Definitely, if he negotiates hard on some of these things, we could see a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach than we have in the past," said Catherine Beard, head of industry association \uc2 Business NZ\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MILLION-DOLLAR HIT\par \par \uc2 The idea is a quota-system would force more logs to be sold and processed locally, adding value to the NZ$270 billion economy.\par \par \uc2 The industry could process around 20 percent more than the current 8 million cubic meters each year with more reliable supply, said Kevin Hing, deputy director of the New Zealand Timber Industry Federation, which represents sawmillers.\par \par \uc2 "We recognize that log export is an option for many forest owners, but we don't want that to be at the expense of domestic processing," Hing said.\par \par \uc2 Forestry owners say big companies already favor local processors and the proposals would have a disproportionate impact on small forest growers.\par \par \uc2 They worry the prospect of curbed earnings would lead growers to hold off planting, reducing the supply of logs.\par \par \uc2 "The unintended consequence of that, which I don't think \uc2 Winston Peters\uc2  has thought through, is that you actually devalue that asset and you chase investors away," said Peter Clark, CEO of forestry service firm PF Olsen Limited.\par \par \uc2 He said such quotas would knock hundreds of millions of New Zealand dollars off timber assets. New Zealand's privately-owned forests, mostly pinus radiata that grow much faster than in their native California, are worth NZ$25-NZ$30 billion.\par \par \uc2 "OPEN FOR BUSINESS"\par \par \uc2 Peters has also said foreign ownership restrictions would be a large part of this week's negotiations talks.\par \par \uc2 He called the high concentration of foreign ownership "crazy" in an August speech on his plans for the forestry sector. Just over half of the country's forests are owned by offshore companies, according to the New Zealand Institute of Forestry.\par \par \uc2 "NZ First, in government, will seriously tighten up the ability of foreigners to buy our land," Peters said August.\par \par \uc2 All of this is making some overseas investors nervous.\par \par \uc2 "They're saying if it gets much harder they think that their head office offshore will start looking at New Zealand ... and say: 'Do we, long term, want to continue investing in here or do we slowly start withdrawing?'," said Warwick Searle, the head of agribusiness at investment consultant CBRE.\par \par \uc2 An official for state-owned China Forestry Group, which owns about 22,000 hectares of New Zealand forest, said overseas ownership was already carefully regulated by the country's authorities and quotas would also make it harder to operate.\par \par \uc2 "We hope that New Zealand remains open for business," Steve Walker, the firm's local chief operating officer, in an email.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Charlotte Greenfield and Ana Nicolaci da Costa; Editing by Lincoln Feast) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-10-10T09:08:04.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171010edaa002ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
