\par\par \b \uc2 NZ First leader welcomes politically driven drop in NZ$\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2017\par 03:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * NZ First leader says exporters should welcome \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall               \par \par \uc2 * Kiwi has dropped 3.7 percent since Sept. 23 poll               \par \par \uc2 * Third day of talks on formation of next government               \par \par \uc2 By Charlotte Greenfield and Ana Nicolaci da Costa               \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Oct 10 (Reuters) - The leader of the small nationalist party that will decide New Zealand's next government said on Tuesday that exporters should welcome a recent fall in the local dollar following an inconclusive general election.               \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar, or kiwi, fell to its lowest levels since late May on Monday after a final tally of votes at the weekend showed the opposition Labour-Green bloc gaining ground over the ruling National Party, although the latter still has the largest number of seats.               \par \par \uc2 The final tally led to market speculation that New Zealand First, whose support is needed by both parties to gain enough seats to govern under the country's proportional representation system, would be more comfortable joining forces with the centre-left Labour-Green bloc, prompting investors to sell New Zealand assets.               \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar has dropped around 3.7 percent since the election on Sept. 23, hitting a four-month low of low of $0.7052 on Monday. It was trading at $0.7063 on Tuesday afternoon.               \par \par \uc2 "I think exporters will be pleased, we are an export-dependent nation," NZ First leader \uc2 Winston Peters\uc2  told reporters after a meeting with Labour, when asked about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline.               \par \par \uc2 NZ First favours greater \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was the 11th most traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world in 2016.               \par \par \uc2 Peters is holding the third day of substantive negotiations with both Labour and National, ahead of his self-imposed deadline of Thursday to announce which party he plans to support. He has in the past served under both National and Labour governments.               \par \par \uc2 Asked whether the talks had included any policies aimed at keeping the NZ dollar down, Peters said: "Well if you're an export dependent nation why would you persist with an inflated dollar, which even the \uc2 IMF\uc2  (\uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 ) says is over-valued? Why would you just ignore all the best advice in the world? But then again this is not just an economic matter."               \par \par \uc2 NZ First has more policies in common with Labour than the centre-right, raising jitters in the market about a a change in policies after National's decade in power.               \par \par \uc2 Labour supports some changes to the mandate of the RBNZ, lobbying for employment to be added to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation-targeting mandate. Both parties also favour curbs on immigration, foreign ownership and the renegotiation of certain trade deals.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-10-10T02:51:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171010edaa0015y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
