\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-New Zealand party leaders meet with caucuses to start negotiation talks\b0\par\par\uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2017\par 07:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Labour says has reached out to New Zealand First\par \par \uc2 * Labour says ready to talk to them when they are ready\par \par \uc2 * National courting NZ First since election night (Recasts with party meetings, updates throughout)\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Sept 26 (Reuters) - New Zealand's major party leaders met with their caucuses on Tuesday, preparing to kick off talks on forming the next government with a nationalist party that holds the balance of power after an inconclusive weekend election.\par \par \uc2 The ruling National Party won the most seats in Saturday's general election, but failed to secure enough to form a government, leaving the New Zealand First Party in the position of kingmaker.\par \par \uc2 Deputy leaders of both the ruling National and opposition Labour parties said they would be willing to give up the role of deputy prime minister to win the backing of \uc2 Winston Peters\uc2 , who leads New Zealand First.\par \par \uc2 But the Labour Party, whose support spiked after \uc2 Jacinda Ardern\uc2  became leader last month, also has a chance to govern in New Zealand's proportional representation system, if it can woo Peters into a coalition along with the Green Party.\par \par \uc2 Ardern said she had "every chance" to become the country's youngest prime minister in modern history, adding that her team had contacted that of Peters, a veteran politician who has served in National and Labour governments.\par \par \uc2 "We are willing and available when he is ready," Ardern, 37, told reporters in Wellington. "I respect the time that Mr. Peters will need to talk with his party."\par \par \uc2 Asked about possible sticking points in talks, she said budget responsibility rules would apply in any government Labour led.\par \par \uc2 Both National and Labour are expected to stick to fiscal prudence, but to differ on monetary policy, trade and immigration. Labour wants to cut immigration and renegotiate some trade deals that could hurt two key sources of growth.\par \par \uc2 It wants to add employment to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation-targeting mandate, which could make monetary policy more stimulatory, and there is some concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  First's preference for more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The political uncertainty weighed on the New Zealand dollar , which was down 0.32 percent at $0.7243, after posting its biggest daily drop in more than four months on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Forming a coalition government could take weeks, Prime Minister Bill English has said, though he hoped talks would be constructive, even if expected to be tense.\par \par \uc2 English has courted Peters since election night, when he acknowledged his "strong performance", saying voters had given him a role in the new government's formation.\par \par \uc2 "He understands his role and the role here is, now that we've had an election where the people have spoken... then it's up to the political party leaders to form the kind of government indicated by the election," English told media.\par \par \uc2 The National Party secured 58 seats, ahead of the 52 won by the Green Party and Labour in Saturday's election, leaving both still in need of NZ First's nine seats to reach the tally of 61 required to form a government.\par \par \uc2 A final tally of election results is due on Oct. 7, when "special votes", which constitute 15 percent of the total and include overseas votes, are released. English remains prime minister in the interim. (Reporting by Ana Nicolaci da Costa in Wellington and Jane Wardell in Sydney; Editing by Clarence Fernandez) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-9-26T06:40:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170926ed9q007bz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
