\par\par \b \uc2 Australia dlr at 1-mth high after construction data; NZ$ muted\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2017\par 08:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, Aug 30 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar climbed to a one-month high on Wednesday after second quarter construction spending rose the most on record, a surprising outcome that could lift economic growth well above initial expectations.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was up at $0.7985, a level not seen since Aug. 1 and compared with Tuesday's $0.7954.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie hit a two-year peak of $0.8066 in late July but has since faltered at stiff chart resistance around 80 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 It is poised to end August almost flat as investors remained cautious amid geopolitical tensions involving North Korea and in the absence of any major triggers.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie got a strong boost from Wednesday's data showing construction work done surged 9.3 percent in the second quarter, from the first. That was the largest increase ever and dwarfed forecasts for a rise of just 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 "At this stage today's data suggests that there are upside risks to our 0.5 percent second-quarter GDP forecast," said Kristina Clifton, economist at Commonwealth Bank.\par \par \uc2 Australia's second-quarter gross domestic product data is due Sept. 6.\par \par \uc2 Across the Tasman Sea, the New Zealand dollar held steady at $0.7260 but slipped to a 5-1/2 low against its Australian counterpart.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under some selling pressure after outgoing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Graeme Wheeler\uc2  emphasised the need for a lower local dollar to help push up inflation and rebalance growth.\par \par \uc2 "In conjunction with stronger Australian data, this was enough to spur AUD/NZD to within touching distance of the 1.10 level, its highest point since March of this year," \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar has broadly decreased since late July as softening economic indicators and uncertainty over the outcome of its general election weighed on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors also pushed back their bets on the timing of the next rate hike after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its neutral stance at the August policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi is set to end the month down 3.3 percent, the biggest fall since January 2016.\par \par \uc2 Speculation that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has also taken the shine off the kiwi, although analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nowhere near taking that path.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand government bonds slipped, sending yields up 3.5 basis points at the long end of the curve.\par \par \uc2 Australian government bond futures inched lower, with the three-year bond contract 5 ticks down at 97.970. The 10-year contract fell 4.5 ticks to 97.3350. (Reporting by Swati Pandey and Ana Nicolaci da Costa; Editing by Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-8-30T07:13:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170830ed8u0071p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
