\par\par \b \uc2 Australia, NZ dlrs gain as greenback sputters\b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 04:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, March 20 (Reuters) - The Australian and New Zealand dollars rose on Monday as the greenback weakened and investors unwound previous bets the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would hike U.S. interest rates at an aggressive pace this year.               \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar inched 0.25 percent higher to $0.7719, a level not seen since Feb. 23. The Aussie made hefty gains last week, rising 2 percent to post its best weekly performance since Jan. 13.               \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar climbed 0.33 percent to $0.7036, its highest since March 2. The Kiwi also posted strong gains last week, up 1.3 percent.               \par \par \uc2 "Though the bounce was a reflection of U.S. dollar weakness, the Aussie is also getting a double whammy benefit from high commodity prices," said Greg McKenna, chief market strategist at CFD and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider AxiTrader.               \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week delivered an interest rate hike, as anticipated, but did not alter its earlier forecast for a total of three rate increases this year. That disappointed dollar bulls who had hoped for hints of a possible fourth hike and for more aggressive forecasts for next year.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese iron ore futures last week boasted their biggest weekly gain in two months underpinned by strength in steel prices.               \par \par \uc2 "The big question is whether the Aussie can kick higher or whether 77 cents is going to continue to be a graveyard for the bulls," said McKenna.               \par \par \uc2 The Aussie has faced stiff resistance in the $0.7700/7740 band since early February and has repeatedly failed to break above.               \par \par \uc2 There was limited initial reaction to the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  meeting over the weekend which retained the familiar form of words on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but dropped a pledge to avoid trade protectionism.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds gained, sending yields across the long-end of the curve down 4 basis points.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-3-20T04:30:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170320ed3k0053d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
