\par\par \b \uc2 RBNZ-Joint statement on Declaration by TPP Macroeconomic Policy Authorities\b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2015\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2015, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Release date - 06112015\par \par \uc2 Gabriel Makhlouf, Secretary to the Treasury and \uc2 Graeme Wheeler\uc2 , Governor of the Reserve Bank of New Zealand today welcomed the completion of a Declaration that will strengthen cooperation between the macroeconomic authorities in Trans Pacific Partnership (TPP) countries, including New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 TPP parties, at the instigation of the United States Treasury, have been working on the Declaration. The \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  requires the US Administration to demonstrate progress on the principal negotiating objective of avoiding \u8216\'20\'18unfair\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices before passing legislation to enable TPP.\par \par \uc2 The Declaration is an agreement between agencies within each TPP country that deals with macroeconomic policy. It does not create any binding legal or political obligations for New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 Parties to the Declaration commit to maintaining exchange rate regimes that reflect economic fundamentals and refrain from competitive devaluation. These commitments are consistent with New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s policy settings and line up with the commitments New Zealand has made as a member of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 . The Declaration will not restrict New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s ability to adjust our macroeconomic policy in future.\par \par \uc2 More broadly these commitments could help support stronger trade between TPP countries and seek to avoid practices that are harmful to economic growth and financial stability.\par \par \uc2 The joint Declaration also requires the release of a range of macroeconomic information and data to support dialogue and improve transparency. The Reserve Bank and \uc2 Statistics New Zealand\uc2  comply with this section of the Declaration.\par \par \uc2 The joint Declaration sets up regular dialogue on macroeconomic and exchange rate policy issues, with the aim of sharing information and understanding the impact of policies on other countries.\par \par \uc2 As a small open economy we are aware that other countries\u8217\'20\'19 macroeconomic choices can affect our own economy. We also recognise that macroeconomic stability plays an important role in supporting stronger trading and investment flows.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s sound macroeconomic policy settings and open and transparent approach to data are consistent with the Declaration. The Treasury and the Reserve Bank look forward to the opportunity to deepen dialogue with our trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Why was the Declaration negotiated in the context of TPP?\par \par \uc2 This was one of a number of issues that the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  has required the US Executive to demonstrate progress on, before it will consider passing the legislation necessary to implement TPP.\par \par \uc2 Does New Zealand comply with the Declaration?\par \par \uc2 Yes. New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s practice and policy is consistent with the elements set out in the Declaration.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand has a freely-floating exchange rate, an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  operating monetary policy with the goal of stabilising inflation, and limited and targeted exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Our macroeconomic policy settings and frameworks are based on delivering macroeconomic stability with a view to supporting trade and investment.\par \par \uc2 Would the Reserve Bank\u8217\'20\'19s policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  breach the Declaration?\par \par \uc2 No. The framework does not restrict the ability of the RBNZ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The framework setting out the operating of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy under Section 16 of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act provides for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the exchange rate is exceptionally high or low and clearly unjustified by economic fundamentals. This is consistent with the text of the Declaration.\par \par \uc2 Is this Declaration legally binding?\par \par \uc2 No. This is an understanding among our macroeconomic agencies. It is not a treaty among TPP governments.\par \par \uc2 Will this restrict New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s ability to change its exchange rate regime or approach to monetary policy?\par \par \uc2 No. The text on exchange rates largely echoes New Zealand existing commitments under Article IV of the IM Articles of Agreement.\par \par \uc2 The exchange rate elements of the Declaration - for example the avoidance of exchange rate manipulation - are helpful in supporting stronger trade.\par \par \uc2 More information: Joint Declaration of the Macroeconomic Policy Authorities of Trans-Pacific Partnership Countries\par \par \uc2 Media Contact:\par \par \uc2 Angus Barclay, External Communications Adviser,\par \par \uc2 Ph 04 471 3698, 027 337 1102, angus.barclay@rbnz.govt.nz\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020151106ebb600095\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
