\par\par \b \uc2 RBC Awaiting Pullback in USD/CAD -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2014\par 13:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:49 EDT - While the US dollar spiked to RBC's C$1.1350 profit target yesterday and it's sticking with its C$1.15 year-end objective, waiting for a pullback to C$1.11-1.12 is RBC's favored strategy right now instead of piling back in at current elevated levels. Meanwhile, RBC says NZD/USD looks like a good sell at current levels given the high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . RBC also calls USD/JPY still the most-highly correlated pair to equity returns, so if risk aversion persists, another clearout of stale longs may be seen. Lastly, the investment bank calls EUR/USD more complicated as positioning is heavily short, which argues for risk of a further unwind. But peripheral pressures are on the rise, a setup which argues for limited exposure in EUR/USD. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EDT - After rebounding some an hour ago, US stock-index futures have slid back, with Dow-industrial futures off 150. There's nothing new on the news front--just another bout of profit-taking following late Wednesday's equity rebound in the US. That reversal helped erase nearly all of Treasurys' early gains, but that market is back in rally mode this morning with the 10-year yield down to an even 2%. Meanwhile, oil falls anews, with \uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude off 2% and making a fresh run at breaching $80. The dollar is broadly higher, with the euro pulling back to $1.2750. Gold is down slightly. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:16 EDT - Wall Street shares finished well above yesterday's lows, but European equities took little comfort as they were hit by a fresh round of selling. Overall, there is a deep sense of concern that investor confidence has dried up, creating an environment in which news that would otherwise be ignored can trigger selling. One example is the Ebola outbreak, as the actual, direct impact on the developed world is likely to be very small. The market is fearing this fear. Meanwhile, in realm of more realistic risks, global deflation is rearing its ugly head. Concerns about that threat were reinforced by a weak eurozone inflation print for September. And peripheral eurozone sovereign bond yields started to rocket again--those on Greek 10-year bonds shot up to 8.8% from 6.6% a couple of weeks ago. (alen.mattich@wsj.com and michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1057 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The extent of the recent reversal in the dollar move means that it will take another period of consistent data improvement, and \uc2 Fed\uc2  messaging, for markets to confidently return to pricing in rate hikes from mid-2015, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says. The dollar index has dropped about 1.8% this week, and strategists expect weekly jobless claims Thursday to bounce off the very low levels reported last time. \uc2 BNP\uc2  has closed its EUR/USD short, making a 1.1% gain. "We will look to re-enter these trades but only when US front-end yields have begun to recover and the risk environment has shown signs of stabilization." (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0000 GMT (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--Downside scope to 84.217 is still on offer on the U.S. Dollar Index chart, despite the bout of USD strength Thursday's European session. Wednesday's push into three-week lows below 84.937 extended the bear wave from the October 3 peak at 86.746, to generate a downwave equality target at 84.217. Bears need to meet that 84.217 objective in order to validate the decline from 86.746, and a break below Wednesday's low at 84.472 is required to make the 84.217 target achievable. Projected resistance at 85.546 would have to be recaptured before labelling the 84.472 low as a pending bear failure, and confirmation of a bear failure would only occur on a break above Wednesday's high at 86.011. The U.S. Dollar Index is at 85.260.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Francis Bray, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\f28 \uc2 \f2  Analyst For Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1035 GMT The huge move in government bonds over the last few days is mainly a symptom of the market having become mispriced and investors continuing to pay too high a price for low return, says Ewan McAlpine, senior client portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal London Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Poor economic data is to blame for some of the move in bonds, but the market has also suffered a fall in liquidity due to changes in some investors' risk appetite, which has led them to cut their positions and in turn has led to further lower yields," he adds. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1034 GMT The cost of insuring against default debt issued by some of Europe's lowest-rated firms continues to climb Thursday as global growth concerns weigh on riskier assets. Markit's iTraxx Europe Crossover index of mainly sub-investment-grade companies is 24bps wider at about 423bps, extending Wednesday's roughly 30bp widening. The weakness pushed the extra yield investors demand for holding euro-denominated high-yield bonds to the highest level in a year Wednesday. Investors now demand 413bps of extra yield relative to a benchmark based on the rate banks charge to lend to each other, Markit data show. They had demanded roughly just 300bps of extra yield as recently as June.(ben.edwards@wsj.com @debtnerd) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1033 GMT French investors are jittery after a Spanish bond auction went sour and the situation in Greece became "pretty shaky", a Paris-based trader says. He adds the market players' mood towards bank stress tests due in two weeks has changed. News about the Ebola fever spreading and sluggish world economic growth also fuel market nervousness, he adds. CAC-40 is down 2% at 3861.34 points. (inti.landauro@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1032 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market turns to its old favorite as eurozone peripheral tensions rise and stocks take another tumble. EUR/JPY sees a quick 1% decline to a fresh 11-month low of 134.15, when USD/JPY sheds 50 sen to a fresh day low of 105.53. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1032 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alberto Gallo\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of European macro credit research at RBS says it's time for markets to wake up and smell the coffee. "Markets have been comforted by the holding hand of central banks for the past five years. Credit investors had become so used to the 'buy-on-dip' trade that many stopped questioning that this holding hand could at some point disappear," he says. He adds that anyone expecting that a turn from five years of loose monetary policy would go through without volatility is foolish. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1030 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chris Leung, an economist at Singapore-based brokerage DBS Group, says the short-term impact of the mass democracy protests will likely be less severe than the 2003's outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome, or SARS, as most parts of the city's economy "are still functioning amid mild disruptions". He expects growth of Hong Kong's gross domestic product to remain flat in the fourth quarter, based on an assumption that the movement ends within this month and travel warnings and restrictions are removed soon afterwards. He also notes that the impact of demonstrations on the retail sector could be easily overestimated. "The sector was hurting long before the recent demonstrations" due to China's economic slowdown and its anti-corruption drive. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1020 GMT The euro seems to be catching up with the selloff seen in other markets as peripheral bonds are hammered and the main European bourses turn sharply lower Thursday. EUR/USD is down 1% at 1.2720, EUR/JPY sinks to a fresh 11 month low of 105.54, EUR/GBP down 0.8% at 0.7966. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 16, 2014 07:49 ET (11:49 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141016eaag001k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
