\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie climbs, brushing off weak Australian jobs reading\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2014\par 04:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie climbs, brushing off weak jobs data\par \par \uc2 * Investors question jobs data following major revisions\par \par \uc2 * Ongoing U.S. dollar selling boosts Aussie, kiwi\par \par \uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Oct 9 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar edged up on Thursday, boosted by broad selling in the U.S. dollar and as investors ultimately shrugged off a weak reading of Australia's labour market after major revisions to the series cast doubt on the data's reliability.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie rose 0.2 percent to $0.8857, bouncing back from an initial slide to $0.8791 after official figures showed the Australian economy lost a net 29,700 jobs in September, wrongfooting expectations for a 20,000 increase while the jobless rate ticked up to 6.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 While the data showed that the labour market is struggling to improve, investors quickly bought back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as many questioned the robustness of the figures after the country's statistics body radically revised past readings due to an absence of seasonal patterns this year.\par \par \uc2 "The market has lost credibility in the data for now. The Aussie briefly went down, before coming back as common sense prevailed," said a trader at a European bank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "The market is just not sure which way to go. Forget the number," he said adding that he expected the Aussie to consolidate around $0.8840.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar tracked the Aussie higher, pushing up 0.3 percent to an intraday high of $0.7938.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie extended gains into a fourth session, pulling further away from a four-year low of $0.8642 hit late last week as many investors book profits on the U.S. dollar's month-long rally versus most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was supported after investors sold the greenback after minutes released on Wednesday from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's latest policy review prompted some investors to pare back expectations that U.S. interest rates may rise by mid-2015.\par \par \uc2 Still, many investors expect that the Aussie will struggle to rise further towards $0.9000 in the near term given that economists believe the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  may hold off from raising interest rates until 2016 as the economy has been slow to recover from a slowdown in the mining sector.\par \par \uc2 KIWI REPRIEVE CONTINUES\par \par \uc2 Broad selling in the U.S. dollar on Thursday extended the kiwi's reprieve from a dramatic two-month sell-off which has left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lingering in range of a 14-month low of $0.7708 hit last week.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi has lost nearly 11 percent since July as investors have dumped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on falling global dairy prices, a pause in the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary tightening cycle and kiwi-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While many in the market expect the kiwi's downward trend to continue, ANZ analysts said that the U.S. dollar's stumble in reaction to Wednesday's FOMC minutes suggested that the kiwi's downward momentum may slow in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 "This further supports the view that we are currently consolidating last month's NZD sell-off with the potential for a counter-trend rally which could surprise with its size," ANZ analysts said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "We see some potential for NZD to pop back above 80 (U.S.)cents in the short term."\par \par \uc2 Still, many analysts expect that any gains above $0.8000 will be short lived, and that the kiwi will weaken further as expectations for U.S. rate rises heat up in the coming months. Some in the market see the possibility of a fall towards $0.7500 before the year is out.\par \par \uc2 Australian government bonds edged up, tracking gains in U.S. Treasuries and pushing three-year bond futures 5 ticks higher to 97.380. The 10-year contract rose the same amount to 96.655.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand government bonds also rose, nudging yields 3 basis points lower across the curve.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Cecile Lefort in Sydney; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-10-9T03:09:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141009eaa9001jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
