\par\par \b \uc2 POLL: Australia & NZ dollars seen falling further\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2014\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * For results see\par \par \uc2 * Aussie seen at $0.8500 in 12 months vs $0.8800 in Sept poll\par \par \uc2 * NZ$ forecast cut sharply to $0.7500, from $0.8050 in last survey\par \par \uc2 By Cecile Lefort\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Oct 2 (Reuters) - Analysts have cut their forecasts for the Australian and New Zealand dollars in the latest Reuters poll as a sharp decline in commodity prices combined with a strengthening U.S. dollar put an end to months of relative calm.\par \par \uc2 A poll of 51 analysts put the Aussie at $0.8500 by this time next year, from $0.8800 in the September survey. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not hanging around, however, having already hit an 8-month trough this week. It stopped a whisker away from its lowest in four years.\par \par \uc2 It was last at $0.8801, having dropped more than 6 percent since last month's survey, in part on global economic growth concerns and sliding iron ore prices. At home, patchy data also reinforced expectations the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) will keep interest rates at record low of 2.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Paradoxically, the more the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slides the less need there might be for a rate cut. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has argued for a long time that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was too high given falling prices for some of Australia's key commodity exports.\par \par \uc2 A drop in the Aussie will boost earnings from commodity exports that are priced in U.S. dollars, while easing financial conditions in the economy.\par \par \uc2 While one analyst at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  saw the Aussie falling potentially as far at 69 cents, the poll's majority was far more restrained with the median forecast at $0.8750 in one-month retreating to $0.8600 in six months.\par \par \uc2 A separate survey of 43 analysts showed an even sharper downgrade for the New Zealand dollar, with the median forecast putting it at $0.7500 in 12 months, from $0.8050 in the previous poll.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi has already tumbled 6.5 percent in September to hit a 14-month trough of $0.7708.\par \par \uc2 It moved a leg lower this week after data showed the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which it views as unjustifiably high given falling prices for some of New Zealand's major commodity exports.\par \par \uc2 The poll put the kiwi at $0.7800 in one month and $0.7700 on a six-month view, from its current level of $0.7884. Views were wildly divergent as usual, with forecasts as low as $0.6500 and as high as $0.8800. (Reporting by Cecile Lefort)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-10-5T07:00:04.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141002eaa200693\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
