\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips but set to end September with solid gains\b0\par\par\uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2014\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar index could mark best monthly performance in over a year\par \par \uc2 * Kiwi, Aussie up on the day but among worst monthly performers\par \par \uc2 * Markets still keeping an eye on developments in Hong Kong\par \par \uc2 By Lisa Twaronite and Ian Chua\par \par \uc2 TOKYO/SYDNEY, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The dollar took a breather on Tuesday from its recent rally but was still not far from four-year peak against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and could be on track to post its biggest monthly gain in over a year.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index last stood at 85.544, not far from an overnight peak of 85.798 - a high not seen since July 2010. Its monthly gain of more than 3 percent could put it on track for the largest since February 2013.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts cautioned that its three-month long rally was at risk of running out of steam for now, particularly against the yen.\par \par \uc2 "It's really hard to pick a bottom, but it does look to us like it's gone a little too far, and has overshot," said Sue Trinh, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "It's like trying to catch a falling knife, in terms of the other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment, and bear in mind it's month-end, quarter-end, and, for the Japanese, half-year end, too, so flows tend to be a little more noisy than usual around that period," she said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar scaled a fresh six-year high of 109.75 yen on Monday and last traded at 109.30 yen, down about 0.2 percent but up nearly 5 percent for the month.\par \par \uc2 Investors continued to warily watch developments in Hong Kong, where tens of thousands of pro-democracy protesters extended a blockade of Hong Kong streets on Tuesday, stockpiling supplies and erecting makeshift barricades ahead of what some fear may be a push by police to clear the roads before China's National Day.\par \par \uc2 RALLY'S ROOM TO RUN?\par \par \uc2 Over the past three months, the dollar index has surged more than 7 percent, driven in part by expectations the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will start to hike interest rates well ahead of its European and Japanese counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Data on Monday showing U.S. consumer spending accelerated in August supported the upbeat outlook for the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields have risen in line with the gradually improving U.S. economy, with the two-year yield nearing 0.6 percent, a high not seen since May 2011. That in turn has bolstered the appeal of the dollar against its lower-yielding peers.\par \par \uc2 The euro came within a whisker of its November 2012 trough of $1.2661 on Thursday before edging back to $1.2695, up about 0.1 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the dollar's rally still had room to run, as the key U.S. nonfarm payrolls report on Friday will likely underscore that the U.S. economic recovery has enough momentum for the \uc2 Fed\uc2  to hike interest rates sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  revised its end-2015 dollar forecast to 115 yen from 112 yen, citing the likelihood of the \uc2 Fed\uc2  raising U.S. rates sooner than expected, strong outflows as Japanese investors rebalance portfolios and the disappointing pace of Japanese export recovery.\par \par \uc2 "Divergence in monetary policy between the \uc2 Fed\uc2  and BOJ should result - as it has resulted - in more foreign security investments as part of Japan's portfolio rebalancing," Shusuke Yamada, chief Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BAML in Tokyo, said in a report.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone inflation data due later on Tuesday will be closely watched by euro bears, but a bigger-than-expected rise in Germany's annual inflation could potentially help keep the euro zone rate stable.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar, down around 6.5 percent this month, was one of the worst performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September.\par \par \uc2 Data on Monday confirming the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  proved the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could talk the talk and walk the walk.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi last stood at $0.7805, recovering about 0.5 percent on the day, having plumbed a near 14-month low of $0.7708 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Its Australian peer has similarly fared poorly, suffering a drop of more than 6 percent this month, a vicious turnaround for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had been trading in a remarkably stable 92-95 cent range.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie fell out of favour as market volatility picked up following the end of the summer lull and as carry trades lost their lustre.\par \par \uc2 China's patchy economic growth and further declines in the price of iron ore, Australia's biggest export earner, then become excuses for selling the Aussie.\par \par \uc2 Momentum sellers joined the fray as key chart support levels gave way, handing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a one-way ticket to its 2014 trough of $0.8660 set in January.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie fell as far as $0.8682 on Monday but last recovered about 0.5 percent on the day to buy $0.8758. (Editing by Shri Navaratnam and Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-30T06:19:54.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140930ea9u004lc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\