\par\par \b \uc2 Australia, NZ dlrs set for largest monthly fall in over a year\b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2014\par 05:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie & kiwi on track for largest monthly losses since May 2013\par \par \uc2 * Aussie down 6.5 pct so far in Sept, NZD off 7 pct\par \par \uc2 * Political unrest in HK adds to investor worries\par \par \uc2 By Gyles Beckford and Cecile Lefort\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The Australian and New Zealand dollars clawed their way off lows on Tuesday but still seemed destined to suffer losses exceeding 6 percent for the month amid a strengthening U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sliding global commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar struggled up to $0.8727, having slipped as far as $0.8684 overnight, its lowest since January. It has tumbled 6.5 percent this month, the largest loss since May last year.\par \par \uc2 While some dealers see a risk of a correction, \uc2 Ray Attrill\uc2 , global co-head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 , does not forecast any meaningful rally.\par \par \uc2 "I don't see a spike above 90 cents in the short-term and I think we will test 85 cents at some point during Q4," he said. He reviewed his Aussie forecast on Monday, keeping it at 88 cents by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Major support was found at the 2014 trough of $0.8660 and a break would take it to its weakest level in more than four years.\par \par \uc2 The Antipodean \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have come under pressure in recent weeks due to a rising U.S. dollar as investors see a greater risk the U.S. Federal Reserve will start hiking rates, albeit not for some time, in contrast to loose policies in the euro zone and Japan.\par \par \uc2 Undermining the Aussie and kiwi dollars was a sharp decline in commodity prices combined with concerns about the health of China's economy, a key export market for both Australia and New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 Spreading pro-democracy protests in Hong Kong only added to worries with investors wary about China's response. The Aussie is often used as a liquid proxy for Asian bets.\par \par \uc2 14-MONTH LOW\par \par \uc2 It was much of the same story for the New Zealand dollar which took a pasting on Monday after data confirmed the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during August to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The kiwi was licking its wounds at $0.7766 on Tuesday, having hit $0.7708, the lowest in 14 months. It has fallen more than 7 percent this month and is down 12 percent from a three-year high of $0.8839 touched in mid-July.\par \par \uc2 Still, the scale of the rout has left the kiwi oversold on technical indicators.\par \par \uc2 "There are good reasons for the sharp move lower to be modestly retraced in the near-term, with the potential to take a look back at $0.80," BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Raiko Shareef said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi is expected to find chart support at the latest low around $0.7710 and more substantial support at the 2013 trough of $0.7680, while resistance was put at $0.7810.\par \par \uc2 However, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 's readiness to act on its words, as it did in August, should keep investors vigilant in case the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seizes any opportunity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The selling in August could well mark the beginning of that campaign, and we would not blink should the next RBNZ update show a similar scale of selling in September," said Shareef.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi was still looking soggy against all the majors. The Aussie dollar was holding the previous day's gains at NZ$1.1225 , while the kiwi was about 0.2 percent down against both the yen and the euro.\par \par \uc2 There was no support from local data as home building consents were unchanged in August, while a survey showed business confidence falling to a two-year low.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand government bonds were trading a touch softer, sending yields half a tick higher along the curve.\par \par \uc2 Australian government bond futures rose, with the three-year bond contract up 2 ticks to 97.250. The 10-year contract added 3 ticks to 96.500. (Editing by \uc2 Simon Cameron-Moore\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-30T04:11:40.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140930ea9u002pg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
