\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Hong Kong protests slow U.S. dollar rally\b0\par\par\uc2 510 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 21:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar stalls near multi-year highs against yen, euro               \par \par \uc2 * Hong Kong demonstrations raise worries about Beijing response               \par \par \uc2 * Kiwi at lowest in over a year as RBNZ data confirms \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Speculators renew bullish bets on greenback last week-CFTC data   (Updates market action)               \par \par \uc2 By Richard Leong               \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 29 (Reuters) - The dollar's three-month rally took a breather on Monday on nervousness over Beijing's response to democracy protests in Hong Kong and concerns it might hurt the region's economy and status as a global financial hub.               \par \par \uc2 The Communist government, which resumed its rule over the former British colony in 1997, made clear it would not tolerate dissent, and warned against any foreign interference.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar's rally on expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve would raise interest rates in the mid-2015 was seen as overdone and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was headed for a pullback, analysts said.               \par \par \uc2 "The situation in Hong Kong is starting to weigh on people. The dollar just has had this massive rally. You are beginning to see some profit-taking," said \uc2 Boris Schlossberg\uc2 , managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at BK Asset Management in New York.               \par \par \uc2 The greenback reached six-year highs against the yen in Asian trading, while the dollar index hit a four-year peak before scaling back on worries about Hong Kong and a sell-off on Wall Street.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.1 percent on the day at 109.42 yen in late U.S. trading. It was down as low as 109.14 yen from an earlier peak of 109.74 yen, according to Reuters data.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar index slipped 0.06 percent at 85.591 after rising to 85.798 earlier.               \par \par \uc2 The euro steadied against the greenback at $1.2691, up 0.06 percent from late Friday. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a two-year low against the dollar earlier Monday at $1.2665.               \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar was stable after falling to a six-month low. Pegged to the U.S. dollar, it was last down about 0.1 percent at 7.7672.               \par \par \uc2 Monday's data on personal spending and pending home sales suggested the U.S. economy is growing but not fast enough for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise policy rates from its near-zero range before mid-2015.               \par \par \uc2 While recent data have signaled the world's biggest economy is far from robust, they reinforce the idea the \uc2 Fed\uc2  is preparing a rate "lift-off" next year, a move that supports a stronger dollar. Data released late Friday showed speculators rebuilt their bullish positions on the greenback last week.                              \par \par \uc2 Chicago Federal Reserve President \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  on Monday urged "patience" on increasing rates, even though he suggested the rates could rise in the not-too-distance future.               \par \par \uc2 The biggest mover among developed-world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. Figures showed the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August in a bid to help its exporters.          