\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand \highlight\uc2  Kiwi Lags as Data Show RBNZ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:26 EDT - NZD/USD has been the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past session after the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction figures show it sold NZ$521M in August, says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. "However, after its precipitous fall, we are wary of a bounce...particularly if the USD uptrend runs out of momentum." Pair down 1.25% at 0.7766. Martin says the upcoming session's domestic focus will be the release of the ANZ business confidence survey for September. She now sees a clear band of support at the mid-2013 lows around 0.7690. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:17 EDT - \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  expects \uc2 Bill Gross\uc2  to attract some $50B in additional assets to Janus (JNS) by the end of 2017 reversing the company's organic growth forecast from -4% to plus 5% a year over the next three years. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says BlackRock (BLK) looks best positioned to benefit from Pimco outflows, though it may come up against overlapping customers looking to diversify their holdings. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  maintains its $17.25 price target on the shares, which end down 7.5% at $14.70, trimming some of Friday's 43% rally. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:47 EDT - So much for the Bill Gross-driven selloff. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  prices up broadly today as haven demand trump the fallout from Gross's abrupt departure from Pimco, announced Friday. "The Treasury bond market is very large...I am confident that the bond market will move beyond this news as it has so many other noteworthy events," says \uc2 James Sarni\uc2 , senior managing partner at \uc2 Payden & Rygel\uc2 , which manages about $85B. The 10-year note is 12/32 higher, yielding 2.490%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 15:18 EDT - \uc2 Dallas Fed\uc2  leader \uc2 Richard Fisher\uc2  says in an interview with WSJ today that he'd like to see the \uc2 Fed\uc2  consider raising rates in the springtime, rather than the later summer period favored by officials at the center of the decision-making process. Fisher shrugs off the recent round of weak inflation data and says a lot of the softness comes from supply rather than demand-related factors. "Does it worry me that we are not at our 2% intermediate-term target? It does not" because what's being seen now in the data represents "benign price stability," he says. Fisher is an FOMC voter and a long-time opponent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easy money policy stance. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:49 EDT - The US 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yield falls to its lowest level in more than two weeks. Stocks in Hong Kong sank by nearly 2% overnight and the selling spread to equity markets in Europe and the US as Hong Kong police clash with protesters. "At this point the heightened tensions is providing just the latest distraction for the market that has been on edge due to other geopolitical concerns," says Adrian Miller at \uc2 GMP Securities\uc2 . "Depending on how the protests advance, the situation in Hong Kong could increasingly become problematic for mainland China and the government's ability to institute reforms while also boosting growth." Ten-year notes up 12/32, yielding 2.490%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 14:32 EDT - A U.S. judge will decide this afternoon whether Argentina should be held in contempt of court, which could open a whole new can of worms in this debt case. The holdout creditors want the judge to impose a fine of $50,000 a day, and this is not unprecedented: U.S. judges have ordered similar contempt sanctions for Russia and the Democratic Republic of Congo. The problem is that court-ordered sanctions would raise questions about a New York judge setting U.S. foreign policy, and no one can really force Argentina to comply with the sanctions. But if the judge doesn't punish Argentina for circumventing his orders, will that make sovereign nations less likely to comply with U.S. rulings in the future? (nicole.hong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:29 EDT - \uc2 The Fed\uc2  says it will make interest payments on bank reserves a primary part of its strategy for raising rates in the future, but some observers tell the WSJ that could be a political minefield. As the \uc2 Fed\uc2  raises rates, those payments will increase. Since the \uc2 Fed\uc2  remits most of its profits to the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2 , the higher payments will lower the revenue the \uc2 Fed\uc2  sends to taxpayers. As former St. Louis Fed President William Poole tells the Journal, "It's highly likely that some politicians will notice that and given the proclivity of some politicians anyway to demagogue issues, the \uc2 Fed\uc2  is going to have some political explaining to do." WSJ's analysis also shows those payments are increasingly going to foreign banks, which could raise eyebrows on Capitol Hill. (ryan.tracy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:22 EDT - \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  lowers its year-end euro forecast to $1.25 from $1.30. With EUR/USD sliding below $1.30 earlier this month, last at $1.2689, the bank says the ECB's efforts to fight deflation will be a driving force. While the firm leaves it's dollar-bloc forecasts unchanged, it maintains that the 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yield should rise to 3.1% by year-end and support the dollar against the euro. That means the bond market can expect to see a "sizeable selloff" in the coming months and contribute to further EUR/USD declines, even if the ECB delays moving into outright government bond purchases. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 14:18 EDT - S&P says it will be hard for the Canadian economy to sustain stronger growth without a pickup in employment, and lists some concerns in recent trend. The ratings firm is worried that only 20% of the 81k jobs added in the 12 months to August were full-time. Canada is also seeing the lowest participation rate in a decade--a sign that jobs are harder to find and discouraged workers have stopped looking. Meanwhile, job creation is barely keeping up with labor force growth, so the jobless rate hasn't progressed in breaking below 7%, much less to the 5.9% pre-recession low. Also, the annualized increase in average hourly earnings has been stuck around 2%-2.5% since 2012. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 13:57 EDT - Brazilian steelmaker Usiminas (USIM3.BR) shares carrying heavy losses into afternoon trading as investors continue to fret about the unexpected firing of CEO Julian Eguran and two other executives last week. A dispute between Usiminas' two controlling shareholder groups, led by Japan's Nippon and Argentine-Italian steelmaker \uc2 Ternium\uc2 , prompted the ouster. \uc2 Ternium\uc2 , Eguren's former employer, said it "vehemently disagrees" with the decision, noting that Usiminas' Ebitda margins and debt indicators improved markedly during the executive's two and a half years at the company. Usiminas recently down 6% at BRL6.69, after falling 5.8% on Friday. (paul.kiernan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:42 EDT - Expect the dollar to weaken a smidge with some month-end selling, stemming from the rebalancing of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedges by equity and bond market investors, according to \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  quantitative strategist Aroop Chatterjee. Those are expected to include some dollar selling versus the euro and yen, and some buying against the Canadian and Australian dollars. "The large \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements have offset the relatively large decline in US equities, thereby reducing the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging in aggregate," he writes. Dollar up 0.1% versus the yen to Y109.42. Euro flat against at $1.2690. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:30 EDT - Monthly manufacturing jobs gains are expected to revert to an average of 12,500 to 15,000 for the rest of the year, says Chad Moutray, chief economist for the National Association of Manufacturers. Moutray also says he wouldn't be surprised if the August reading of zero jobs gains is revised higher. September nonfarm payrolls should exceed 200k, he adds, compared to 142k in August, which he expects will also be revised upward. Moutray also expects real GDP growth of 3.3% in 3Q. (clare.Ansberry@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 29, 2014 16:26 ET (20:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t003hu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
