\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand \highlight\uc2  Kiwi Lags as Data Show RBNZ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:26 EDT - NZD/USD has been the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past session after the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction figures show it sold NZ$521M in August, says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. "However, after its precipitous fall, we are wary of a bounce...particularly if the USD uptrend runs out of momentum." Pair down 1.25% at 0.7766. Martin says the upcoming session's domestic focus will be the release of the ANZ business confidence survey for September. She now sees a clear band of support at the mid-2013 lows around 0.7690. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:23 EDT - \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says the declining student-loan default rate that the Department of Education recently posted is a credit positive for US higher education. Improving figures lesson some risks that accompany high default rates, like reputational damage for schools, loss of access to federal financial aid programs and accreditation sanctions, the ratings firm says. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  expects default rates to decline during the next few years, though "pockets of risk will persist" at community colleges, for-profit institutions and historically black colleges and university. (melissa.korn@wsj.com; @melissakorn)\par \par \uc2 16:13 EDT - Moral victory today for bulls, who were able to recover the lion's share of steep opening losses by the closing bell. The \uc2 Dow\uc2  Industrials also avoided a sixth straight triple-digit daily move after falling almost 180 points shortly after the open. Some of the nervousness in equities markets gets pinned on pro-democracy protests in Hong Kong, though the lurching moves during the past few sessions shows investors can be fickle when eyeing expensive stock markets. Materials, energy and financials were the biggest laggards, while utilities finish higher. DJIA falls 41.93 to 17071.22, and \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Comp sheds 6.34 (earlier off about 48) to 4505.85. S&P 500 ends 5.05 lower at 1977.80. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:55 EDT - \uc2 Ford Credit\uc2 , the finance arm of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  (F), says today that subprime auto lending has returned to the levels seen in 2007, but loan losses remain consistently healthy. The company says 15% of loans qualify as subprime -- a rate not seen since before the financial crisis. Ford will now begin offering 75-month terms on loans, a new high in terms of length of maturity. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:51 EDT - Boutique researcher MoffettNathanson's first ratings on network operators Level 3 (LVLT) and Cogent (CCOI) are a buy. The firm reasons that both telcos, despite very different business models, should see free cash flow "surge" in coming years as they skim more profitable business customers off incumbents providers' base. LVLT could also save hundreds of millions by refinancing debt. Still, fears of network connection disputes and LVLT's unwieldy footprint have kept both companies' multiples relatively low. The firm suggests both are bargains as it pins a $43 price target on CCOI and forecasts $57 for LVLT. Bandwidth discounter CCOI gains 1.8% to $34.23, while much larger LVLT jumps 3% to $45.98. (andrew.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @drewfitzgerald)\par \par \uc2 15:47 EDT - So much for the Bill Gross-driven selloff. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  prices up broadly today as haven demand trump the fallout from Gross's abrupt departure from Pimco, announced Friday. "The Treasury bond market is very large...I am confident that the bond market will move beyond this news as it has so many other noteworthy events," says \uc2 James Sarni\uc2 , senior managing partner at \uc2 Payden & Rygel\uc2 , which manages about $85B. The 10-year note is 12/32 higher, yielding 2.490%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 15:39 EDT - The dollar store saga over Family Dollar (FDO) is "far from over," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says, as it calls on investors to be patient over the next couple months as the discount chain decides between being sold to \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  (DLTR) or \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  (DG). FDO needs more "downside protection" before cozying up to DG because should both deals fall apart, FDO would likely trade around $59, more than 23% off current levels. The firm expects DLTR to up its "last & final" offer in the coming weeks, but still is betting on DG to prevail in this fight. FDO nearly flat at $77.52. (paul.ziobro@wsj.com; @pziobro)\par \par \uc2 15:30 EDT - Ford expects European volumes to grow to 16 million to 17 million by 2020, with weakness in Russia and Turkey expected to be near-term. F's Europe Chief said regulatory pressure will remain, and a pricing battle will last through the rest of the decade. The executive said growth in Russia has been killed by geopolitical pressure, but growth will resume in 2016 and Russia still has potential to be Europe's largest market. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:18 EDT - \uc2 Dallas Fed\uc2  leader \uc2 Richard Fisher\uc2  says in an interview with WSJ today that he'd like to see the \uc2 Fed\uc2  consider raising rates in the springtime, rather than the later summer period favored by officials at the center of the decision-making process. Fisher shrugs off the recent round of weak inflation data and says a lot of the softness comes from supply rather than demand-related factors. "Does it worry me that we are not at our 2% intermediate-term target? It does not" because what's being seen now in the data represents "benign price stability," he says. Fisher is an FOMC voter and a long-time opponent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easy money policy stance. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:04 EDT - \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 's (F) head of North America \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Hinrichs\uc2 , says the company expects the US auto industry to keep chugging along, reaching 17M to 18M annual sales by 2020. The industry is on pace to hit about 16.5M in light-vehicle repairs, the highest rate in several years. Ford also says it expects growth in the auto industry in 2015, with volumes in the 16.8M to 17.5M range. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:00 EDT - Traders are talking about a sudden price spike in \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  (MCD) that hit at 2:44 pm EDT. A sudden wave of volume carried MCD from about $95.30 to as high as $97, or about 1.8% in just a few minutes. MCD now at $96.21, or gain of 1.6%. The spike put MCD trading volume at 170% of 30-day average volume and at its peak price was a more than two-month high. No MCD news on the tape. (christopher.dieterich@wsj.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 14:52 EDT - A cool front appearing over the northern Midwest in the next two weeks is signaling the start of Fall temperatures, and the natural gas market is taking notice. Natural gas futures jumped more than 3% on Monday to their highest settlement since July 9, finally breaking out of a $3.70-$4.10 range it had been stuck in since mid-summer. Analysts said the market got off to a bullish start with the debut of November as the new front-month contract Monday as well as the appearance of lower temperatures that could drive heating demand, but momentum took over once the market broke through prior technical resistance. November futures gained 12.5 cents to settle at $4.154/mmBtu. (christian.berthelsen@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 29, 2014 16:26 ET (20:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t003ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
