\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: The Challenges of Governing in a Weak Global Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 1,774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 13:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey and Alen Mattich\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    WRAP:   The challenges of governing in a weak global economy were on display Monday. Most strikingly, the protests that have gripped Hong Kong speak to the difficulty that China has in trying to sustain the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Communist Party\f28 \uc2 \f2 's centralized control over power and the economy while also seeking to take a leadership role in the global economy. But it's also evident in much smaller \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been forced to heavily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after its earlier rate hikes attracted funds into its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , funds that had been created by bigger central banks. And in Europe, the forces of deflation stirred by regional and global economic stagnation showed their influence in consumer price data from Germany and Spain. (MC) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG: Pro-democracy rallies that had spontaneously arisen in different parts of Hong Kong Sunday grew bigger Monday as supporters from around the territory joined the students and members of the Occupy Central movement in protesting new Beiing-controlled plans for electing the territory's chief executive. Businesses, including the banks that manage Hong Kong's powerful financial center, suspended some services and activated backup contingency plans as the streets around the government buildings in Admiralty and shopping districts of Causeway and Mong Kok teemed with protesters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The immediate lesson in these events is for the Hong Kong police, which appear to have created a much bigger problem for themselves by resorting to tear gas early on in the protest, which stoked sympathy and support for the protesters and denied them the opportunity to use force to disperse an even bigger crowd. The bigger lesson lies in the tensions fueled by China's attempt to exert more political control over the territory and, by extension, with the challenges the mainland authorities face in trying to maintain a centralized, undemocratic political system while at the same seeking to create a modern, internationalized economy. Hong Kong is the conduit to those international aspirations. Notwithstanding the local business community's opposition to the Occupy Central protest movement, its long-term success hinges on the kind of openness and political freedom that the protesters are seeking to maintain. (MC)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K.: August mortgage approvals were 64,212 from 66,100 in July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Is the British housing market slowing down? \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists have been projecting softness in the second half of the year and mortgage approvals were slack in August, though maybe this was just a seasonal blip. (AM)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SPAIN: September consumer price index fell 0.3% on the year from a 0.5% fall in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain continues to suffer deflation. Mass unemployment continues to keep a lid on price pressures as do global factors like falling commodity prices. But, so far, it's not quite the sort of debt deflationary spiral economists fear. (AM)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMANY: September regional consumer price indices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --BAVARIA was up 0.1% on the month and up 0.8% on the year\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --BRANDENBURG was up 0.1% on the month and up 0.7% on the year\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --NORTH RHINE WESTPHALIA was unchanged on the month and up 1.1% on the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German inflation remains subdued. With the economy struggling for growth and commodity and oil prices falling, the disinflationary pressures seem firmly established. (AM)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANCE: Ruling Socialists lose grip of the upper house of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Parliament\f28 \uc2 \f2  after the weekend's election.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's socialists go from bad to worse. The latest round of elections represents the third electoral defeat for the party in the space of six months. With the government's reform program sputtering, there seems no likely end to the economy's struggles anytime soon. (AM)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 INDIA: An extravaganza with Bollywood dancing and thousands of cheering Indian Americans greeted Indian Prime Minister Narenda Modi Sunday as he kicked off a visit to the U.S. that will see him visit President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barack Obama\f28 \uc2 \f2  later Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The event, which was marked by a stirring speech, garnered much attention in the Indian press as it appeared to encapsulate a sense of hope that the country's charismatic new Prime Minister might be able to transform a nation whose aspirations have been held back while its neighbors have grown. (MC)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW ZEALAND: The New Zealand dollar touched a 15-month low after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  revealed that it sold a net 521 million New Zealand dollars (US$406.4 million) in August--the most since July 2007--which encouraged speculation that more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be forthcoming.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBNZ's actions highlight the challenges that monetary authorities in small economies face while the major central banks of the world are engaged in far-reaching monetary stimulus programs. With the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  both still buying bonds and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  planning to do so, while interest rates are near-zero for so many others, it's very hard to maintain independent policies for both interest rates and exchange rates. Earlier this year, the New Zealand \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been one of the first advanced-economy central banks to raise rates since the financial crisis, as its economy continued to grow on the back of agricultural exports, but that made its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a magnet for all the global liquidity unleashed by the bigger central banks. As the New Zealand dollar rose against the Australian dollar and other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it created competiveness challenges for local businesses and eventually drew the RBNZ into the fray. (MC)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMING UP:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMANY: 8 a.m. EDT (2 p.m, Berlin) September provisional consumer price index. [Expected -0.1% on-month vs. unchanged in August; expected +0.8% on-year, in line with August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk of deflation in Germany is arguably the strongest factor driving the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  toward quantitative easing--mostly because it's the one thing that could dissuade the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bundesbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  from aggressively resisting that shift to bond-buying. The earlier regional data have already confirmed that this risk is alive and well. (MC)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S.: 8:30 a.m. EDT. Personal income and outlays for August. [Personal income expected +0.3% vs.+0.2% in July; personal spending expected +0.5% vs. -0.1%; PCE price index expected unchanged on-month vs. +0.1% in July.]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Personal spending is bouncing around a bit, but for the most part it's explained by seasonal noise and other effects. Overall, the income and spending picture points to steady but not necessarily robust growth. Perhaps the most important component to watch is the personal consumption expenditures (PCE) index, to which the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  pays close attention as its main inflation indicator for the purpose of setting its 2% target. After showing signs of rising earlier this year, it has slipped back into a disinflationary trend. Softening energy prices and food prices, coupled with a stronger dollar, which lowers the cost of imports, should sustain that trend for some time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S.: 10 a.m. \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Association of Realtors\f28 \uc2 \f2  August pending home sales. [Expected unchanged on month vs. +3.3% in July.]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last month's gain was taken as a welcome affirmation of steady growth in this leading indicator of future residential property sales. The index is currently at 105.8, comfortably above the 100 level that real-estate industry specialists say denotes a "normal" level of activity. So, even if the report shows no change, as expected, it should be seen as evidence that the housing sector continues to recover, and that has all sorts of spinoff benefits for the rest of the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S.: Time N/A. Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Evans\f28 \uc2 \f2  gives speech at \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Association for Business Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2  annual meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Evans showed his well-established credentials as one of the Federal Open Market Committee's leading doves last week, arguing passionately against any hasty rush to raise rates. For one, he's worried that a stronger dollar will drive inflation and growth even lower. Importantly, Mr. Evans will be a voting member the FOMC next year, right when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  is expected to prepare for its first increase in the federal funds rate in eight years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K.: 7:05 p.m. EDT (12:05 a.m. Tuesday, London). GfK September consumer confidence survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recent data have suggested that the spring and summer's pickup in retail sales has waned, mostly because customers are uncomfortable with the economic outlook. Here's an opportunity to gauge how that sentiment looks going forward. If spending further declines, it could lead the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  to hold back on its momentum toward what many believe will be a rate hike sometime in the next 12 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JAPAN: 7:50 p.m. EDT (8:50 a.m. Tuesday, Tokyo)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --August preliminary industrial production. [Was +0.2% on--month in July.]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The headline number last month seemed to signal more gloom and doom for this key indicator of Japanese all-important manufacturing sector. However, some of the more forward-looking components of the report offered glimmers of hope that demand and production are picking up. There was a 2.3% on-month decline in inventory to shipments, for example, and company forecasts revealed and expected acceleration in output over the next two months, presumably to restock those waning inventories. Japan desperately needs this sector to pick up, so policymakers will be looking to see if this latest result confirms that this happening.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --August preliminary retail sales. [Previously were +0.5% on-year.]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese households still aren't spending. The economy needs them to get shopping.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHINA: 9:45 p.m. EDT (9:45 a.m, Beijing) \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC China\f28 \uc2 \f2  final manufacturing PMI. [Preliminary September "flash" PMI was 50.5 vs 50.2 at end-August.]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 29 Sep 2014 07:25 ET \par Macro Horizons: The Challenges of Governing in a -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The flash number came as a relief to investors as it defied expectations for a drop to 50, which would have put the indicator right on the threshold of expansion versus contraction. But there have been plenty of other signals in Chinese data suggesting the economy is slowing. If this end-month readout were to adjust the preliminary results downward, there could be a worldwide blow to sentiment. (MC) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 29, 2014 07:25 ET (11:25 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t001cr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
