\par\par \b \uc2 Challenges of Governing in a Weak Global Economy -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 13:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:25 EDT - The challenges of governing in a weak global economy were on display Monday. Most strikingly, the protests that have gripped Hong Kong speak to the difficulty that China has in trying to sustain the \uc2 Communist Party\uc2 's centralized control over power and the economy while also seeking to take a leadership role in the global economy. But it's also evident in much smaller \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been forced to heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its earlier rate hikes attracted funds into its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , funds that had been created by bigger central banks. And in Europe, the forces of deflation stirred by regional and global economic stagnation showed their influence in consumer price data from Germany and Spain. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1116 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Thailand's Finance Ministry\uc2  is less optimistic about this year's economic growth after official data show the country's exports continued to contract for a second month in a row. "Now that we expect the export growth rate to be less than 1% this year, it is unlikely that the 2014 GDP growth will be as high as 2.0%," says Kritsada Jinavijarana, director-general of the ministry's Fiscal Policy Office. "In this case, the Thai economy will likely grow 1.6%." The Finance Ministry in July forecast export growth of 1.5% -- the rate that will support economic growth of 2.0% in 2014. The ministry is scheduled to announce its latest GDP growth project in October, Mr. Kritsada adds. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: HHO \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1028 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Consumer confidence in India significantly improved in September over the previous month, said analytics firm ZyFin Research in a report Monday. As the Hindu festivals Diwali and Dussehra approach, sales of homes and automobiles are likely to rise, says ZyFin. After "muted festive season sales in 2012 and 2013, the latest readings of the index point towards a departure from this trend", says Debopam Chaudhuri at ZyFin in a statement. ZyFin calculates a Consumer Outlook Index based on a monthly survey of 4,000 consumers in 18 Indian cities. The index rose to 45.2 in September, a 1.6-point increase from the previous month. A score above 50 reflects optimism, and one below 50 reflects pessimism--but ZyFin is hopeful that the upward trend will continue in India. Some consumer companies saw their stock jump--shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Procter & Gamble Hygiene & Health Care\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500459.BY) climbed 5.57% to 5401 rupees Monday. Marico's (531642.BY) shares rose 1.94% to 297.05 rupees Monday. (shanoor.seervai@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1016 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--US Treasurys tread water ahead of a busy week of events, culminating in the monthly nonfarm payrolls release on Friday, as data continue to hold sway. "Last Friday's selloff may have been fuelled by the noise around the personnel reshuffling at Pimco but the upward revision in Q2 GDP - while in line with expectations - also was a reminder about improving underlying economics in the US. The bearish macro impetus is likely to prevail," say analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Ahead, personal income/spending at 1330 GMT and pending home sales at 1500 GMT. December \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  futures are 2/32 higher at 124-1160 and the 10s yield 2.527%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1009 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Headlines in Greece this week will be dominated by Tuesday's arrival of representatives from the country's international creditors who will launch talks on whether Athens has done enough to be paid its next slice of aid, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alpha Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The arrival of the troika this week will be in the spotlight," the brokerage says, referring to representatives from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Last week's meeting between the (Greek) PM and the German Chancellor had little to offer from an economic viewpoint as the German side offered political support." Earlier this month, Greek officials concluded a first round of talks with the troika in Paris regarding payment from its 240 billion euro rescue package. Negotiations with troika are expected to run until late in the year and will include whether Greece insert in its 2015 budget a series of tax breaks to boost the economy. (stelios.bouras@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0957 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Allianz\f28 \uc2 \f2 's bonds could struggle in the coming weeks if the resignation of Pimco founder \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bill Gross\f28 \uc2 \f2  leads to investors pulling more cash out of the asset management firm's funds, says Eva Olsson, credit strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi UFJ Securities International\f28 \uc2 \f2 . An outflow of retail cash--which makes up about a third of Pimco's $1.5 trillion of third-party assets under management--could also spur volatility in more illiquid areas of the debt market if \f28 \uc2 \f2 Allianz\f28 \uc2 \f2 -owned Pimco needs to sell bonds to meet redemptions, she says. That could turn the spotlight on the changes Mr. Gross's exit means for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Allianz\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which could in turn cause the insurer's bonds to underperform, she notes. (ben.edwards@wsj.com @debtnerd) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0955 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The chances of Hong Kong falling into a technical recession, defined as two quarters of contraction, in the third quarter is "pretty high," said Raymond Yeung, senior economist at ANZ. He said China's slowing economy is the main reason for this, but Hong Kong's political turmoil doesn't help matters. "If you have a strong retail sector that continues to be vibrant, that would provide some offset to the slowdown in cross-border trade," but the city's turmoil raises fears that retail sales will be affected during a vital shopping period, he said.(kathy.chu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0952 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesian shares close higher, recovering slightly after shares fell sharply Friday in the shadow of an unpopular parliamentary vote to scrap regional elections. The JSX index rose 0.2% to 5142. Consumer goods company \f28 \uc2 \f2 PT Charoen Pokphand Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CPIN.JK) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 PT Bank Central Asia\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BBCA.JK) each rose 2.6%, among the day's top gainers. The day's trading was held back by the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to analysts at HD Capital. "Rupiah weakness above the 12000 mark capped further gains in the index," the brokerage says. The U.S. dollar rose 1.3% against the rupiah to 12175 late in the day, according to FactSet data. A broadly stronger U.S. dollar and political concerns are battering Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JVW \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0949 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The chances of Hong Kong falling into a technical recession, defined as two quarters of negative growth, in the third quarter is "pretty high," says Raymond Yeung, senior economist at ANZ. Yeung says China's slowing economy is the main reason for this, but Hong Kong's political turmoil doesn't help matters. "If you have a strong retail sector that continues to be vibrant, that would provide some offset to the slowdown in cross-border trade," but the city's turmoil raises fears that retail sales will be affected during a vital shopping period, he says. (kathy.chu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0948 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  says its third-quarter economic growth forecast of 1.8% for Thailand could be at risk if export and import data continue to lag. Exports fell 7.4% on year in August, worse than the market had expected. Imports fell 14.2% on year because of the overhang effect of large inventories. August's $1.1 billion trade surplus was largely the result of this sharp fall in imports, and indicates that the economy isn't ticking along as it should. "Between the trade surplus and downside GDP risk, the latter, with a net portfolio investment effect, could have a larger impact on THB sentiment," Citi said in a research report. (james.hookway@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0944 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/PHP advances to a six-month high, tracking broad USD gains on U.S. growth that was stronger than anticipated. The pair closed at 44.995 versus 44.72 on Friday. "The dollar should get an additional boost if U.S. [job and manufacturing] data due this week point to even stronger growth," says a local bank trader. Traders expect the USD/PHP to move between 44.80 and 45.20 in the near term.(cris.larano@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JVW \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0943 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Cambodia's plan to sustain its 7% annual growth and increase its investment beyond 25% of GDP depend on the country's integration with other members of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Association of Southeast Asian Nations\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 ASEAN\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) and continued close ties with China, says Moody's Investors Service. Cambodia's real GDP growth has likely slowed slightly in 2014 thanks to political uncertainty that undermined confidence and labor unrest. The country's B2 sovereign bond rating faces constraints posed by the small size of the economy--with a nominal GDP of $15 billion--which offers little shock absorption capacity. Low per-capital income and rapid credit expansion are also challenges, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  report. (warangkana.chomchuen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 29, 2014 07:26 ET (11:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t001al\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
