\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar surges anew, euro zone data eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 10:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar hits new highs against yen, euro, overall \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket               \par \par \uc2 * Kiwi at lowest in over a year as RBNZ data confirm \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Hong Kong dollar falters after pro-democracy demonstrations               \par \par \uc2 * CFTC data show speculators increased dollar bets               \par \par \uc2 * Euro under pressure before data, ECB meeting this week               \par \par \uc2 By \uc2 Patrick Graham\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 29 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to new multi-year highs against the yen, euro and a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, a three-month-old rally showing no signs of dissipating before a week of important economic set pieces.               \par \par \uc2 Employment numbers on Monday preface the month's biggest release in the United States, non-farm payrolls. They are expected to drive home the message sent by gross domestic product numbers on Friday of an increasingly robust recovery.               \par \par \uc2 By contrast, a first estimate of September inflation is expected to show the euro zone economy is stagnating. That divergence has driven the euro almost 9 percent lower since July, to its weakest in almost two years.               \par \par \uc2 The biggest mover among developed-world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian trading was the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. Figures showed the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August.               \par \par \uc2 That, and weekend protests in Hong Kong, added to the sense of a more turbulent period ahead for markets as the U.S. Federal Reserve brings an end to a programme of bond-buying that has pumped trillions of dollars into the global economy.               \par \par \uc2 "I do wonder if there's a bit of risk aversion sliding into the dollar rally now," said Adam Myers, European head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in London.               \par \par \uc2 "If we do see a bit of a back-up in performance in emerging markets, then we may see people moving towards buying dollars as a safe haven on top of the strength we have seen."               \par \par \uc2 The kiwi was some 1.4 percent lower at $0.7760. The yen was another quarter point lower at 109.55, just off a new six-year high for the dollar of 109.75 yen.                              \par \par \uc2 INFLATION               \par \par \uc2 Data released by the \uc2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2  on Friday showed speculators increased their bullish bets on the U.S. dollar in the week ended Sept. 23. The value of the dollar's net long position rose to $35.81 billion from $31.42 billion the previous week.               \par \par \uc2 "There is definitely the feeling that the dollar is moving towards overbought territory," Myers said. "If we get a weak payrolls number at the end of the week, we may well see a decent correction next week."               \par \par \uc2 Economists expect the report will show U.S. employers hired 219,000 people in September, a rebound from August's rise of only 142,000.               \par \par \uc2 The essence of the dollar's rise since July has been the divergence of the U.S. economy's direction from those of Europe and Japan. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  is beginning to rein years of loose monetary policy; the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are under pressure to do more for growth.               \par \par \uc2 Euro zone inflation is expected to fall to 0.3 percent from 0.4 percent a month ago, underlining that picture ahead of the ECB's monthly meeting on Thursday. The euro reached 1.2664 , its lowest in almost two years, in Asian trade before stabilising in early deals in Europe.               \par \par \uc2 Asian market attention turned to Hong Kong, where democracy protesters defied volleys of tear gas and police baton charges in the financial centre.               \par \par \uc2 The city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the Hong Kong dollar was stable after the tense situation had earlier pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a six-month low in its biggest fall in a single day this year.               \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar, which is pegged to the U.S. dollar, was last up about 0.1 percent at 7.7623 against the greenback.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-29T09:24:25.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140929ea9t0077y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
