\par\par \b \uc2 Barclays\uc2  Urges Caution In Chasing Kiwi -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 09:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0755 GMT 0000 GMT (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 )--"Further New Zealand dollar weakness is likely, but we would be cautious about extrapolating the pace of decline registered over recent weeks into the future as: - Implied volatility has spiked sharply and looks unsustainable. - There is a risk that its decline could morph into a much sharper fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if confidence evaporates. Therefore, it would be prudent for the RBNZ to engineer a more orderly decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . - Around half of the overvaluation has already been adjusted, which leaves less room for depreciation. - Investors have shed long positions, implying that further room for a decline in net NZD longs has been exhausted." Now at $0.7760. katie.martin@wsj.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Italy's new December 2020-dated floating rate note, or CCTeu, which will be launched at auction Monday, can be a good defensive opportunityyfor investors who want to take profit on peripheral bonds at the shorter maturities, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . Italy's \uc2 Treasury\uc2  launches the new instrument with EUR2.5 billion to EUR3 billion as part of an upto EUR8.5 billion bond auction which will also include the reopening of August 2019 and December 2024-dated BTPs. Results are due at 0900 GMT. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0713 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The restoration of India's credit rating outlook to stable from negative by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Friday doesn't mean an upgrade in the rating will take place anytime soon. Economists say \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2 's move takes off pressure of an imminent downgrade to non-investment grade, but an upgrade will take place only after the government takes decisive steps to address persisting problems such as an inefficient farm supply chain and government control over energy pricing. "While some quarters will make a case for a rating upgrade next, the latter is still some distance away as some clarity is still required on the fiscal consolidation efforts, take-off pace of the reform agenda and sustained pick-up in growth in a controlled inflationary environment," says Radhika Rao, an economist at DBS Bank.(anant.kala@wsj.com; Twitter: @anantvkala) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0709 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's current account surplus may have peaked, says Nomura, after August's reading narrows slightly to $7.3bn, down 7.2% from the previous month. The bank says the appreciation of the Korean won, an expansionary budget for 2015 and an improving housing market suggest that the current account surplus should narrow in dollar terms and as a percentage of GDP. "A large net financial surplus in the private sector suggests there is room to cut policy rates to boost domestic demand and therefore reduce the current account surplus," says Nomura. "We maintain our call that \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  will cut policy rates by 25 basis points to an all-time low of 2.00% at its next policy meeting on 15 October." The Kospi falls 0.3% to 2,026.60. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; Twitter: @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0701 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY will likely head toward 120 over the next one to two years, after hitting 112 by the end of this year, says Taisuke Tanaka, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Tokyo, in a note. Japanese economic indicators have shown a tepid recovery from a falloff in consumption after the April sales tax increase, an economic scenario that often creates risk aversion sentiment, causing the JPY to strengthen. But this tendency is being outweighed by the main driver of the USD/JPY uptrend: steady growth in the U.S. economy. The USD/JPY is now at 109.58 after hitting 109.75, its highest in about six years. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0700 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD rally is expected to continue this week with analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  pointing at Wednesday's ISM and Friday's nonfarm payrolls as potential bullish signals. "We expect September non-farm payrolls to return to above-200k growth on Friday with a 215k print which would be spot on the average monthly gain since the start of the year. Meanwhile, the moderate pullback in the manufacturing ISM on Wednesday should still leave the US ahead of the rest of the G10, underpinning bullish USD positioning," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP\f28 \uc2 \f2 . USD/EUR trades at 1.2680. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0659 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese government bonds rise in tandem with improved liquidity conditions as funds return from IPO subscriptions. Five- and seven-year yields are at 3.88% and 3.95%, respectively, versus 3.92% and 3.99%late Friday. The benchmark seven-day repo rate is 2.88% versus 3.30% late Friday. With China's markets about to close for a weeklong holiday, investors are likely to hold their bond positions with nothing bond-negative expected in sight, says a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank. China's financial markets will be closed from Oct. 1 to Oct. 7 due to the National Day holiday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JVW \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0658 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The South Korean won dropped to its weakest in over five months Monday, as the U.S. dollar strengthened to a four year high. Analysts from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in a note Monday they "expect the Chinese economy to slow down moderately in 2015, weighing on exports and the KRW," noting that they expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken. The won hit 1054.20 against the U.S. dollar, compared with Friday's close of 1,044.10. (anjani.trivedi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0657 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Bank of Japan Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  sticks to his rosy price forecast despite a sharper-than-expected slowdown in inflation in August, suggesting he feels little pressure--so far, at least--to boost monetary easing. The inflation rate, as gauged by the core consumer-price index, is "expected to subsequently follow an uptrend again from the second half of this fiscal year and reach about 2% around the middle of the current projection period from fiscal 2014 to fiscal 2016," Mr. Kuroda says in a speech, repeating almost exactly the language he used in early September, well before the release of August consumer price data. The current easing measures are having the "intended effects," he adds. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: HHO \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0654 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wine Estate's (TWE.AU) shares end the day down 8.5% at A$4.50 after takeover talks with private-equity suitors break down. Shares closed off the day's low of A$4.13, but are back near where they traded when the company revealed in May that it had been approached by \f28 \uc2 \f2 KKR\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Akshay Chopra, an investment analyst at Karara Capital, which doesn't have a stake in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says hedge funds that bought shares in hope of a takeover premium were probably dumping the stock. He adds that if private equity firms felt they could turn \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  around, then there's no reason why the company's management couldn't too. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0654 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Australian dollar continued to be crunched Monday, falling to an 8-month low after being caught in a downdraft generated by a plunging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  confirmed it had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in August, moving to protect the country's exporters from sharply falling commodity prices. The Australian dollar has tended lately to be caught up in moves by the New Zealand dollar due to its regional proximity and the fact that it too is a commodity-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Despite a sharp fall through September, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still likely hoping for further falls in the Australian dollar. At 0500 GMT, the Australian dollar changed hands at US$0.8699, compared with US$0.8755 late Friday. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0651 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The South Korea won weakens to a level last seen in April, with dealers buying U.S. dollars for customers whose stop-loss orders were triggered by the breach of the 1,050 round-figure trading barrier. The surge of the U.S. dollar has temporarily stalled near the top of the Bollinger uptrend channel around 1,054, but it could continue over the course of the week. The bullish technical signal of the Bollinger uptrend channel will continue to exert its effect on USD/KRW as long as the pair stays above 1,043 on a daily-closing basis. A similar bullish chart indicator in USD/JPY--a lead indicator for USD/KRW--is another reason to be bullish on dollar-won pair, which closed at 1,053.8 versus its Friday close of 1,044.4. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JVW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 29, 2014 03:55 ET (07:55 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t000o1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
