\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand \highlight\uc2  Dollar Down Late After RBNZ Confirms  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lucy Craymer   \par \uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 06:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON--The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is sharply lower late Monday after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  confirmed it had sold New Zealand dollar and the country's Prime Minister suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be significantly lower.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar has "dropped like a stone" and is "in free fall territory" after NZ's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in August, said \uc2 HiFX\uc2  Senior trader \uc2 Michael Johnston\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Data released by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  showed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold a net 521 million New Zealand dollars (US$404.5 million) in August. This was the largest reduction in New Zealand dollar assets since July 2007 and significantly higher than the NZ$2 million it sold in July.\par \par \uc2 The data is the sum of what the bank has bought and sold during the month and therefore its sale of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be significantly higher than the net figure.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 John Key\uc2 , a former \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, earlier said he supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar should be around US$0.6500. Mr. Johnston said this was also putting some pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar fell sharply on the data and touched US$0.7708--its weakest since late June 2013, from around US$0.7828 just prior to the data. Late Monday in New Zealand, the Kiwi was at US$0.7766 compared with US$0.7891 late Friday. It was at A$0.8917 compared with A$0.9014 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Johnston said the New Zealand dollar could be around US$0.7500 to US$0.7600 by the end of the week, particularly if the U.S. jobs data is strong.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lucy Craymer at lucy.craymer@wsj.com, @lucy_craymer\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 29, 2014 00:39 ET (04:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t000bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
