\par\par \b \uc2 NZD/USD at 12-Month Lows, RBNZ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Unlikely -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,820 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2014\par 07:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0501 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] NZD/USD is at its lowest level in a year after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said the exchange rate is "unjustified and unsustainable" and susceptible to a "significant downward adjustment." While Gov. \uc2 Graeme Wheeler\uc2  said "unjustified and unsustainable are important considerations in assessing whether exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is feasible," he also said "another consideration is whether conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are conducive to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  having an impact on the exchange rate." The pair is at 0.7995 versus 0.8070 prior to the statement. Stuart Ive, Wellington-based client advisor for \uc2 OM Financial\uc2 , says the New Zealand dollar is likely to remain under pressure into the European session. However, he doesn't think immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on the cards because of the jawboning. "You wouldn't talk it lower before you sell it," says Mr. Ive. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Australian dollar has weakened in Asia in sympathy with the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar following "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", by the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Todd Elmer, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citi. "The fact that Aussie can sell off on such a weak catalyst signals that short positioning is not stretched," Elmer says. "On this basis I would expect the Australian dollar to continue to decline moving forward in line with the broader U.S. dollar trend. I think it is one of the most vulnerable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (james.glynn@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @JamesGlynnWSJ) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0433 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  predicts the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2  will keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy stance unchanged at its half-yearly review in October. "Though recent economic activity indicators have disappointed and headline inflation has come in weaker than expected, core inflation remains sticky and above its long-term average," ANZ says in a note. Data released earlier this week showed that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's core inflation measure, which strips out private road-transport and accommodation costs, was at 2.1% on year in August, a tad slower than July's 2.2%. The government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expect core inflation to average between 2.0% and 3.0% in 2014. ANZ says that a tighter labor market poses upside risks to core inflation. The house predicts USD/SGD to reach 1.2800 by the end of the year. USD/SGD was recently at 1.2676 on Thursday vs. 1.2650 late in Asia on Wednesday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0432 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Australian dollar printed a fresh 7-month low Thursday in Asia following news of softer job adverts which were down -0.7% on quarter, says Matt Simpson, senior market analyst at ThinkForex. "We may see a follow-through of today's decline when Europe opens and absorbs the poor job adverts data," Mr. Simpson says. With a lack of domestic news it is now over to the U.S. to provide further A$ movement tonight with employment and manufacturing data due for publication. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0431 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Conflicting factors could keep USD/THB in a consolidation range of 32.14-32.24. The overnight spike of the U.S. dollar index may be providing support for USD/THB, but the improvement in risk appetite triggered by the rebound of U.S. stocks has helped Thai stocks rise 0.2% thereby buffering the baht against weakness. The strength of the USD/THB chart support has increased now that the 20-day Bollinger mid line has converged with the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone at 32.14. If USD/THB ends the day above 32.24, it would be back inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel and likely on its way higher in the near term. In the background, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asian Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's downgrade of its 2014 forecast for Thailand's GDP to 1.6% from 2.9% may be a medium-term negative for the baht. USD/THB is now 32.25 from its last close of 32.21. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0420 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities drift between gains and losses in the morning session without clear leads from the rest of the region. The only conviction seems to be selling Macau casinos. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Galaxy Entertainment Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0027.HK) falls 2.6% to HK$46.80 and Sands China (1928.HK) is down 2.4% at HK$41.55, leading declines. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index falls 0.1% to 23,903.72, while H-shares underperform; the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index sheds 0.5% to 10,665.78. CIMB says it's just market nerves. "In the US, the fear is of rising growth, tightening labour markets and hence, an earlier-than-expected rise in US policy rates," says CIMB. "At the other end of the world, the fear is that growth in China will falter meaningfully. In our view, neither is likely but, yes, the markets will get nervous from time to time." (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0416 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's stock market will get support if Beijing replaces its reform-minded central banker to pursue a more accommodative monetary policy, analysts say. The Wall Street Journal reported Wednesday that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zhou Xiaochuan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , governor of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who has in recent months continued to press for market reforms despite a slowing economy, may be replaced, making way for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guo Shuqing\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a former banker and top securities regulator. "The economy is not doing well and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  should have cut interest rates already. If the reported personnel change leads to lower interest rates, it will definitely help the stock market," said Amy Lin, a senior analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Major blue chips, including banks, property developers and infrastructure firms are expected to benefit from a more supportive \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 , analysts say. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.5% at 2355.91, after touching an intraday high of 2359.80, which marked a fresh peak not seen since March 1, 2013. (hong.shen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0412 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippine government posted a wider budget surplus of PHP29.9 billion in August, buoyed by a 10% increase in revenue collection that outpaced a 5% rise in spending to boost economic activity. Tax collections by the Bureau of Internal Revenue and the Bureau of Customs were up 8% and 11%, respectively. Finance Secretary Cesar Purisima said tax collection could even be higher if the government could soon resolve the congestion in the Port of Manila, where the bulk of the country's imports and exports pass through. Purisima noted that if the port is decongested, "our annual tax collection can increase by as much as an estimated 7% or 0.2 percentage points of GDP." (cris.larano@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0407 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's interest rates will drop if Beijing replaces its reform-minded central banker to pursue a more accommodative monetary policy to support a slowing economy, analysts say. The Wall Street Journal reported Wednesday that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zhou Xiaochuan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , governor of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who has in recent months continued to press for market reforms despite a slowing economy, may step down and be succeeded by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guo Shuqing\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a former banker and top securities regulator. "If the story turns out to be true, the authorities are trying to put in place someone who is more pliant toward [monetary] easing. Greater accommodation would mean lower interest rates across the yield curve," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tim Condon\f28 \uc2 \f2 , economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Condon says market chatter about Zhou's departure were likely behind a decline in China's onshore interest rate swap prices, financial derivatives that hedge against and reflect expectations for interest rate movements. The benchmark one-year interest rate swaps are now at 3.37%, down from 3.56% on September 12 when the recent downtrend began. (hong.shen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0402 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asian Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has cut its projected growth for Thailand this year to 1.6% from 2.9%, according to the bank's update to its 2014 outlook report. In its projection, Thailand will be the worst-performing economy in Southeast Asia in 2014. The country's political turmoil earlier this year is one of the factors contributing to a lower growth forecast for Southeast Asia to 4.6% from 5%, ADB says. However, Thailand's growth forecast next year is maintained at 4.5% and the country's recovery from its slump will help spur Southeast Asian growth. Thai inflation is forecast to come down to 2.2% per year from a previous projection of 2.4%. In 2015, the inflation is expected to rise to 2.6%. Thailand's inflation rate is the second lowest in Southeast Asia after Singapore. (warangkana.chomchuen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0400 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japan's carbon tax should be eliminated, argues the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan Iron & Steel Federation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a trade body. The government already levies a tax on oil and coal, says the industry, adding that the tax is a big revenue generator for the government thanks to the phasing out of nuclear energy in favor of fossil-fuel powered plants. The government garnered 39.1 billion yen ($358 million) revenue from the carbon tax in its first six months of the tariff, which was implemented in October 2012. In the years April 2013 to March 2014, the government had a 75 billion yen increase in its oil and coal tax revenue thanks to rising fossil-fuel demand and a weaker yen. "Even without a carbon tax, the government has secured sufficient additional money," the statement says, adding that the carbon tax contradicts the government's own long-term energy plan it approved in April in which it identified oil, natural gas and coal as important future energy sources for Japan. (mari.iwata@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 25 Sep 2014 01:01 ET \par NZD/USD at 12-Month Lows, RBNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0355 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Consumer demand for major domestic appliances across eight Southeast Asian countries declined by about 3% over the last year, market research firm GfK says. Sales of goods like refrigerators, microwave ovens, washing machines and air conditioners were down in Thailand due to ongoing political turbulence, while electricity and fuel costs weighed on consumers in Indonesia. Although sales remain robust in countries like Laos, Cambodia, Vietnam and the Philippines, demand fell in the region overall between August 2013 and July 2014 compared to a year earlier partly due to the size of the markets in Indonesia and Thailand. (newley.purnell@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @newley) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 25, 2014 01:01 ET (05:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140925ea9p000dw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
