\par\par \b \uc2 New Zealand Dollar Down Late After Early Sell Off\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lucy Craymer   \par \uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2014\par 07:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, New Zealand--The New Zealand dollar was sharply lower late Monday after a sell off in early trading in light liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Markets were divided on what had caused the sell off in the Kiwi with some traders blaming the fall on moves in the New Zealand dollar-Australia dollar cross and broad strength in the U.S. dollar, while others suggested that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a local news website interest.co.nz reporting the speculation. There has been no confirmation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by either the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders who would have been involved.\par \par \uc2 ANZ Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Sam Tuck said there is no concrete evidence the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market but "the move from US$0.8390 down to sub US$0.8350 was driven by volumes that were abnormal, compared to what we would expect to see on Monday at that time."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tuck said he will be watching the RBNZ twitter feed and media page closely to see if there is any confirmation.\par \par \uc2 "Given the RBNZ put the market on-notice at the July OCR (official cash rate), this isn't a rumour you can just ignore," he said.\par \par \uc2 However, ASB Head of Institutional Sales Tim Kelleher said the move came after an "Aussie-Kiwi option barrier was taken out" and was exacerbated by the fact that markets are fairly illiquid early Monday.\par \par \uc2 In late Wellington trading the New Zealand dollar was at US$0.8348 compared with US$0.8413 late Friday and at US$0.8961 versus A$0.9025.\par \par \uc2 The next major event on the calendar is the release of the overseas merchandise trade data due Tuesday. According to a poll of 9 economists by The Wall Street Journal, New Zealand's trade balance is expected to swing into a deficit in July due to softness in exports during the month.\par \par \uc2 The poll sees a goods trade deficit of NZ$500 million in July, with the 12 months trade balance expected to record a surplus of NZ$1.46 billion. The data is not seasonally adjusted and New Zealand exports are significantly impacted by seasonal factors due to the nature of agricultural production. July's exports are expected to be hit by ongoing falls in prices for dairy and wood products.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lucy Craymer at lucy.craymer@wsj.com, @lucy_craymer\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 25, 2014 01:28 ET (05:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140825ea8p00095\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
