\par\par \b \uc2 NZD/USD Will Recover Over Next Few Days: ASB -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2014\par 05:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0333 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The NZD/USD continues around 0.8350 while NZD/AUD remains below 0.9000 at 0.8964 after the kiwi fell early in the session. "It is possible that there was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or it is simply stop-losses being triggered as the USD strengthened further in early Monday morning trade," \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASB Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. U.S. Federal Reserve Chairwoman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  outlined both bear and bull cases for the monetary policy outlook and appeared to be shifting to a more neutral view in her speech at Jackson Hole, ASB said. "The resultant shift in market expectations supported the USD," ASB says. ASB expects NZD/USD to recover some of Monday morning's losses over the coming days. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Asia's exports to China rose 4.5% on year in the second quarter of 2014, according to trade data on 13 countries from CEIC, down from 6.8% in the first three months of the year and the slowest rate of growth since the second quarter of 2012. The region's exports were dragged down by a $1 billion, or 5.3%, drop in exports to China from South Korea. The calculation does not include exports from Bangladesh, Cambodia, Indonesia, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka or Vietnam, which have not yet reported bilateral trade data up to June. (wayne.arnold@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  retains its immediate target of 0.8200 for NZD/USD as "firmer USD nuances continue to emerge despite Jackson Hole proving a non-event," says ANZ Chief Economist Cameron Bagrie. He notes while "geopolitical concerns and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric waxes and wanes, the contrasting economic and QE trends across the G-3 are the key trend to watch and are USD supportive into year-end." NZD/USD is at 0.8351. Bagrie also notes that NZD/AUD has broken lower through the 0.90 level and now just has minor support which finishes at 0.8950 "but it's a move that is looking stretched." NZD/AUD is at 0.8964. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0310 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei is up 0.2% at 15569.57 midday on low volume trading. "The summer doldrums continue, showing that Friday's Jackson Hole (economic policymaker's) summit was somewhat overhyped," says an equity trading director at a European brokerage. "The weaker yen is still not causing any mass short-covering or 'capitulation buying', etc," he says. \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2  research analyst \uc2 Tomo Kinoshita\uc2  points to July U.S. new home sales (due Monday), as well as July U.S. durable goods orders (Tuesday) as the next potentially tradable data points. Domestically, the government will decide to go ahead with another consumption tax hike in Oct. 2015 if real GDP shows growth of at least 3% on-quarter annualized, he says. \uc2 Chugai Pharmaceutical\uc2  (4519.TO) is down 8.9% at Y3,335 after a media report that \uc2 Roche Holding AG\uc2  decided against bidding for the 40% of Chugai it doesn't already own. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China yuan rises despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guiding the yuan weaker via a daily fixing rate. The yuan is at 6.1518 to the dollar compared with 6.1529 at Friday's close. "The yuan weakened a bit when the market opened--tracking the weaker fixing rate. But investor interest in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated later and drove it stronger," a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader says. The central parity is set at 6.1653 compared with 6.1617 Friday. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are at 6.2340/6.2380 from 6.2340/6.2370 late Friday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  cuts BYD's (1211.HK) price target to HK$71.00 from HK$74.00 after lowering 2015 profit estimates by 5% as the company guided a weak 3Q net profit due to sluggish seasonal gasoline car sales. The house says share price is likely to react negatively to this weak guidance as consensus might revise down estimates. It keeps Outperform on BYD as the best electric vehicle play in China. Looking ahead, BYD's electric vehicle sales is likely to further spike on "car purchase tax exemption" list release in September and its new battery plant kick-off. The stock is down 2.9% at HK$47.75(lorraine.luk@wsj.com Twitter @LorraineLuk)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan shares are up 0.1% at 9391.27 on light trading Monday morning, with some traditional industry stocks buoying the market, says a local trader. "Traditional industries are recovering after some weakness," he says. \uc2 Aerospace Industrial Development Corp\uc2  (2634.TW) shares jumps 76.2% to NT$29.70 on the first day of trade for the newly listed government-controlled aerospace company. HTC (2498.TW) rises 4.7% to NT$134.00 on market chatter about upcoming devices. The Taiex is tipped for a 9300-9400 band for the session. (eva.dou@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] BMD CPO hit a fresh five-year low Friday but prices may have already hit the bottom, or are "very close" to the trough due to biodiesel demand from Indonesia, says Indofood Agri-Resources chief executive \uc2 Mark Wakeford\uc2  in a video interview with \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . Attributing the recent slide in palm prices to weak soyoil prices as well as a decline in Brent crude prices in the last four weeks, Mr. Wakeford says the corresponding fall in crude palm oil prices is prompting a pick up in biodiesel demand. "As we go into the peak (palm) season in Indonesia, that surplus capacity that is available to go into biodiesel is already shifting into biodiesel quite quickly so that should keep stocks in check, and at some point as we get beyond the peak season, we should see some recovery in prices," he says. Benchmark November BMD CPO ended 1.9% lower MYR2,000/ton Friday. (huileng.tan@wsj.com; Twitter: @huileng_tan)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's Australian Export Commodity Index has fallen to a fresh 5-year-low in Asia Monday and is now an increasingly difficult fact for the Australian dollar to ignore, says Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . Falling Chinese house prices and data showing soft China manufacturing activity are behind the weakness in things like the iron-ore price. "This is an under appreciated factor for the Australian dollar, which should be having more impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and probably will be in the days to come," Mr. Callow said. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY could test this year's high at around 105.40 on continued dollar strength following Fed chairwoman Yellen's speech at Jackson Hole, says Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Citigroup Global Markets Japan\uc2 . "The current rise in the dollar/yen is not led by yen weakness, but by dollar strength," he says, noting that volatility in the options market remained small, indicating that yen-selling wasn't particularily picking up steam. He says the rise in U.S. interest rates will continue to be reflected in a rise in the dollar/yen. "The pair could fall toward 103.80 on adjustments, but I think it's looking toward testing the high of the year (105.45)," he says. The USD/JPY is at 104.18. (alexander.martin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Australian dollar is likely to be capped by the more positive U.S. dollar following U.S. Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's speech at Jackson Hole, which pundits saw as slightly more optimistic on the job market, though still in no hurry to raise rates, says Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . He notes that in recent sessions the U.S. dollar has reached 4-month highs vs yen, 4-month highs vs sterling, 5-month highs vs the New Zealand dollar and an 11-month high versus euro. Callow said the Aussie's relative resilience amid broad U.S. dollar gains is probably mostly due to its ongoing role as the highest yielding AAA-rated sovereign in a world of super-low yields. "But with commodity prices softening in line with China's weaker data, we agree with Reserve Bank of Australia Governor \uc2 Glenn Stevens\uc2  that there are ongoing risks of a significant fall," he said.(james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei is up 0.3% at 15582.98, benefiting from reflexive futures buying as the dollar rises decisively through the Y104 mark (now Y104.16). "[U.S. Federal Reserve chief Janet] Yellen's comments [at the Jackson Hole conference] were very careful and neutral in nature, and as such did not necessarily tip the \uc2 Fed\uc2 's hand either way with regard to a prevailing stance on policy," says \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2  senior technical analyst Yutaka Miura. "The underlying trend on yen weakening remains unbroken--despite little movement in long-term U.S. interest rates after Yellen's speech. Trading volume is also low, showing that the overall market disposition [upward trending/light participation] has not fundamentally changed." (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 24, 2014 23:33 ET (03:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140825ea8p00080\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
