\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong Stocks Seen Weaker on Earnings -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,141 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2014\par 03:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0104 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities are seen opening the week down slightly with little data for China due and with corporate earnings likely to predominate. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index rose for a second week, gaining 0.6% to 25,112.23 last week, though index futures slide 0.7% ahead of the market open. "The August \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  PMI flash reading in China revealed weaker growth momentum amid tighter monetary conditions and less aggressive policy measures in July, but these are likely to normalize in the current month," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the Chinese yuan "will be one of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  under pressure today as China's growth weakness has finally caught up with its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." Industrial profit data due Thursday will be closely watched, the bank adds. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; Twitter: @gregorhunter)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Despite New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing the official cash rate by 100 bps the impact on borrowers has been muted with the mortgage rate increasing 30bps, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 Economist\uc2 Robin Clements\uc2 . "If the RBNZ is after evidence that the economy is 'adjusting' to rate hikes so far, then we think the evidence is mixed and far from convincing at this point." He adds growth momentum may have already peaked and the favorable inflation results have given the RBNZ the option to hold the cash rate at 3.5%. "How long this pause lasts will... depend upon the inflation outlook and the implications of the next round of GDP, \uc2 CPI\uc2 , HLFS and \uc2 LCI\uc2  data." He adds no change in the cash rate in September looks like a done deal, but it could look different by December. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;Twitter: @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] NZD/USD remains under pressure at 0.8346 after an early morning tumble from 0.8397 to 0.8336 against a backdrop of some unconfirmed speculation that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. ANZ Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Sam Tuck says there is no concrete evidence the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market but "the move from 0.8390 down to sub 0.8350 was driven by volumes that were abnormal, compared to what we would expect to see on Monday at that time." Tuck says he will be watching the RBNZ twitter feed and media page closely to see if there is any confirmation. "Given the RBNZ put the market on-notice at the July OCR, this isn't a rumour you can just ignore." The \uc2 Reserve Bank\uc2  declined to comment. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] New Zealand's trade balance is expected to swing into a deficit in July due to a softness in exports during the month, according to a Wall Street Journal poll of 9 economists. The poll sees a goods trade deficit of NZ$500 million in July, with the 12 months trade balance expected to record a surplus of NZ$1.46 billion. The data is not seasonally adjusted and New Zealand exports are significantly impacted by seasonal factors due to the nature of agricultural production. However, this month's exports are expected to be hit by ongoing falls in prices for dairy and wood products. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;Twitter: @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Iron-ore prices should find reasonable support around US$90/ton, a level they bounced off mid-June when record port stockpiles were acting as a drag on prices, ANZ analysts write in a note. "That said, strong restocking interest [from steelmakers] remains sidelined," the analysts say. Iron ore has fallen on weak Chinese data, including worse-than-expected manufacturing numbers. According to The Steel Index, iron-ore prices fell 2% Friday to US$90.10/ton. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Possible privatization of U.S. air-traffic control services is prompting sharp criticism from business-aviation interests and some federal officials who manage the current system. The extent of opposition to copying changes implemented years ago in Canada emerged at a recent air-safety conference in Washington. Edward Bolton, the FAA official in charge of modernizing the U.S. network, initially stuck to the agency's standard response that it doesn't have a position on privatization. But he immediately noted it took Canada an unusually long time to develop and staff its revamped system, adding "we don't have 10 years" in the U.S. to wait for improvements. Edward Bolden, president of the main trade group representing business aviation, also seemed to open the door for change. But once again, he quickly followed by saying that sometimes, "it's easy to look and say junk what we have" and "do something bold, let's do something radical." Yet in the end, he suggested, sticking with the current structure "may be a better alternative." (andy.pasztor@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0009 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says Santos's (STO.AU) 30% stake in the Lasseter natural-gas discovery off Western Australia state announced Friday may be worth A$270 million--assuming it is between 2 trillion and 4 trillion cubic feet. "With the adjacent Crown discovery and development options including Ichthys train three, Darwin backfill gas or stand-alone floating LNG if more reserves are proved up we think the discovery is in a good address," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says. It increases its price target to A$16.58 from A$16.41. Santos shares were last at A$15.16. (Ross.Kelly@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 Nikkei\uc2  is likely to trend higher following the dollar's surge through Y104 (now Y104.20) over the weekend. Friday comments by U.S. Fed Chief \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  at the Jackson Hole conference that good progress in the labor market could trigger a hike in the federal funds rate target were tempered by the lack of an outright hawkish stance on monetary policy. The dollar nevertheless added to its three-week stretch of gains. "The market will react positively to the dollar's rise, but foreign hedge funds are still looking for an event-driven catalyst that will move the market on more robust terms," says Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. He puts the \uc2 Nikkei\uc2 's range at 15450 to 15600. \uc2 Nikkei\uc2  September futures closed 100 points lower at 15555 in Chicago vs their prior close in Osaka at 15550. In the cash market, the \uc2 Nikkei\uc2  snapped a nine-day winning streak Friday, slipping 0.3% to 15,539.19. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 24, 2014 21:04 ET (01:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140825ea8p0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
