\par\par \b \uc2 NZX-50 Adds 0.4% to 5194.27 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2014\par 07:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0526 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The NZX-50 ended up 0.4% at 5194.27 after it "steadily crept up" over the latter part of the session, Hamilton Hindin Greene broker James Smalley said. He notes there has been a selloff for some stocks, like \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2  (AIR.NZ), which lost 3.0% to NZ$1.92, and \uc2 Ryman Healthcare\uc2  (RYM.NZ), which ended down 1.0% at NZ$8.06. Volumes are light. He said some investors may be taking profits off the table ahead of earnings season or it could be that some Australian investors are opting to repatriate funds given concerns that the NZD might fall heavily if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the other direction, Chorus (CNU.NZ) rose 1.2% to NZ$1.685 as the market was cheered by news that it amended its bank facilities to give it more flexibility. "At least the banks have shown they are willing to come to the party and adjust the covenants," he says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] NZD/USD has stuck to a tight range for most of the session as "it was always going to be about consolidation today as the market takes on board comments from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including overtones that they are willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market if the situation warrants it," says OM Financial client advisor, Stuart Ive. Looking ahead, next week is marked by an "exceptional amount of U.S. data," including GDP, the FOMC statement and employment data and any USD strength will just push it lower. Domestically the GlobalDairyTrade auction will be closely watched and any further weakness in dairy prices will also weigh on the Kiwi, says Ive. The pair is at 0.8578 and Ive tips support at 0.8550 and resistance at 0.8585. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] India shares extend gains to hit fresh high Friday on better results from companies during the quarterly earnings season. The S&P BSE Sensex rises to a new high of 26300.17 and is now trading flat at 26,278.14. The index has gained about 3% in the past one week. \uc2 SKS Microfinance\uc2  rises 3.9% to INR287.20 after its net profit during the first quarter jumps almost 10 times to INR493.2 million. \uc2 Glenmark Pharmaceuticals\uc2  is up 4.5% at INR622.80 as its net profit rises 43%. However, software exporter Wipro falls 5.8% after it earnings just met analysts' expectations. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The July ANZ Business Outlook Survey suggests that confidence in the business sector has continued to moderate from recent exceptionally high levels, but remains high relative to historical experience, says Darren Gibbs, chief economist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New Zealand. He says that while the RBNZ will "be clearly pleased to see some signs that activity indicators are moderating from the exceptionally high levels recorded in recent months, they will need to move further to return to levels that are likely to be sustainable over the medium term." While monetary policy is on hold for a few months, probably at least through the September and October reviews, "we still think it is likely that the RBNZ has further work to do." Gibbs says if there is some rebound in dairy prices and some decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate "this will probably start with one further rate hike before the end of this year at the December meeting." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0402 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Imports to the Philippines in May are down 9.6% year over year--the biggest drop since April 2012. This is a result of a 44% fall in purchases of oil and other fuel products. Data for May show mineral and fuel imports fell to $664 million from $1.18 billion a year ago. Demand last year due to elections and lower prices for oil products have magnified reduced consumption this year because of a truck ban imposed by Manila City Hall and higher oil prices, Wealth Securities research head Ricardo Puig says. "It could be a leading indicator of weaker economic performance in the second quarter," he adds. Philippine GDP growth was 5.7% in the first quarter--below the government's 6.5%-7.5% target for 2014. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The RBNZ decided to increase its cash rate while its Australian counterpart won't because New Zealand business confidence and activity are consistent with 4.0% annual GDP growth by the end of the year or early 2015 while "Australia is barely positive," \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  head of Asian-Pacific research Annette Beacher says. Consumer sentiment in New Zealand "is still in the highly expansive zone while Australia is still sulking about the May budget." Finally, New Zealand inflation expectations at 2.62% are a two-year high. RBNZ Gov. Graeme Wheeler says "it is important inflation expectations remain contained." Racing away from the mid-2% target "is not what the RBNZ governor has in mind," she says. The RBNZ hasn't finished tightening--"it is merely pausing for now," she says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hong Kong-dollar demand is fueled by IPOs and share buying. The USD/HKD is hovering around 7.7504--close to the strong end of the dollar peg and down from around 7.7510 early Thursday. "Hong Kong dollar liquidity has become tighter again and interest rates are squeezed up," a senior trader at a local bank says attributing demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pork company \uc2 WH Group Ltd\uc2 . raising US$5 billion in an IPO and buoyant China data sending Hong Kong stocks to a three-year high. The HKD is repeatedly hitting the strong side of the band and encouraging the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first two weeks of July. "Capital inflows may go on next week," the trader says. (fiona.law@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0343 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China shares are up midday, boosted by strength in banks and cyclical sectors, though analysts warn of a potential correction after recent gains. The Shanghai Composite Index rises 0.5% at 2115.53 and 2150 could act as immediate resistance. Despite the gains, Shenyin Wanguo Securities analyst Li Xiaoxuan says the market may brace for a retreat following recent gains as Beijing's measures to support economic growth may have largely been priced in. Banks gain on low valuations after AgBank said it will tweak its mortgage policy for first-time home buyers in Shanghai, a move seen to support housing-market sentiment. AgBank (601288.SH) is up 1.7% at CNY2.44 while ICBC (601398.SH) advances 0.6% to CNY3.50. Metal plays are higher on the back of stronger metal prices. The Shenzhen Composite Index adds 0.5% at 1106.68. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Chinese government bonds extend gains as liquidity conditions continue to improve. The one-year government-bond yield is 2 basis points lower at 3.74%, and the seven-year government-bond yield is 4 basis points lower at 4.17%, after the benchmark seven-day repo rate is down at 4.10% versus 4.16% late Thursday. But \uc2 Guotai Junan Securities\uc2  warns about upside risks in the bond yields. July data shows the government's loosening policies are starting to take effect and business expectations are improving. Both may lead to a substantial increase in money and credit demand from the real economy and hurt liquidity in the bond market, the securities firm says. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] New Zealand business confidence continued to wane in July, \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 's business outlook survey showed Friday, with a net 39.7% of the respondents in the monthly survey expecting business conditions to improve over the next 12 months compared with a net 42.8% who had expected an improvement in the prior survey. "Business optimism continues to wilt," says ANZ Bank Chief Economist, Cameron Bagrie. However, "the declines need to be put in perspective; we're coming from extreme levels of optimism." Bagrie says that while confidence is 31 points off its peak "the underlying pulse still looks good." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0321 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Singapore industrial output likely contracted at a slower pace in June at 0.8% year over year compared with a 2.5% decline in May as the electronics sector continues to remain weak, according to a median of five estimates in a \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  poll. "Electronics exports have been declining for the past 23 months and the recent 'firm specific' disruption to production is adding salt into the wound," DBS bank says. The electronics sector has been contracting for nearly two years as demand moves away from computers and laptops to tablets and smartphones--forcing component producers to change their product mix. Measured month over month and seasonally adjusted industrial output is likely to have contracted 0.9% compared with a 5.7% contraction in May, according to the median of three estimates. The government will release June data at 0500 GMT on Friday. (venkat.pr@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] McKinsey Global Institute estimates that the growth of Internet-based technologies could account for about 7% to 22% of the increase in China's GDP between now and 2025. Over that time period, McKinsey estimates China's GDP will grow at an average rate of 6.6% and will double in size. Moreover, Internet-based growth isn't the kind generated by heavy capital investment -- meaning the kinds of industries that have turned the sky over Chinese cities brown with smog. The Internet is the very definition of productivity and innovation, McKinsey says, which is just what China needs. Readers, though, should take McKinsey's ode to China with some skepticism. The McKinsey Global Institute is almost always relentlessly bullish on China, even though the country's growth rate continues to slow, even by China's somewhat suspect officials stats. (Bob.davis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 25, 2014 01:26 ET (05:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140725ea7p000fm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
