\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE UPDATE 2-NZ  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  raises rates, seen on hold until December\b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2014\par 05:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Removes extraneous word from headline)               \par \par \uc2 * OCR raised 25 bp to 3.50 pct, as expected               \par \par \uc2 * RBNZ: Appropriate to pause and assess impact of tightening, more rises needed               \par \par \uc2 * RBNZ: NZ dlr high and unsustainable, could have big fall               \par \par \uc2 * NZ dollar falls, interest rate futures rally               \par \par \uc2 By Gyles Beckford               \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, July 24 (Reuters) - New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted its official cash rate by 25 basis points to 3.50 percent as expected on Thursday, but then suspended its rapid-fire run of interest rate rises to study their impact on the economy.               \par \par \uc2 It was the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 's fourth consecutive rise in as many policy meetings, taking the rate from 2.5 percent in March to July's 3.5 percent.               \par \par \uc2 "It is prudent there now be a period of assessment before interest rates adjust further to a more-neutral level," RBNZ Governor \uc2 Graeme Wheeler\uc2  said in a statement.               \par \par \uc2 He said rates would still need to rise in the future, but the economy was feeling the impact of its previous tightening, with commodity prices falling, and inflation moderate.               \par \par \uc2 Consumer price inflation ran at an annual rate of 1.6 percent in the second quarter, \uc2 Statistics New Zealand\uc2  said last week, below the 1.8 percent annual pace forecast in a Reuters survey.               \par \par \uc2 The move to the sidelines was widely expected given the recent fall in some key commodity prices, tame inflation, and strong New Zealand dollar.               \par \par \uc2 The bank's forecasts in June implied the cash rate reaching 3.75 percent by the end of the year, two additional 25 basis point rate increases this year, and a steady pace through 2015.               \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll after the statement reaffirmed the consensus view that the RBNZ will pause until December before resuming rate rises, with a total of 100 basis points of tightening seen over the next year.               \par \par \uc2 "Our view remains unchanged, we think the \uc2 Reserve Bank\uc2  is on hold until December, and they will start raising rates again then, but at a far more gradual pace," said ASB Bank chief economist Nick Tuffley.               \par \par \uc2 Financial markets also scaled back their expectations of rate rises in the next 12 months to 31 basis points from 68 basis points before the statement.                              \par \par \uc2 KIWI DOLLAR BATTERED               \par \par \uc2 While the RBNZ's economic comments largely met expectations, it surprised with its aggressive comments about the exchange rate.               \par \par \uc2 "With the exchange rate yet to adjust to weakening commodity prices, the level of the New Zealand dollar is unjustified and unsustainable and there is potential for a significant fall," Wheeler said.               \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar plunged a full cent to a six-week low of $0.8593 before paring its losses slightly to settle around $0.8600. Interest rate futures <0#NBB:> rallied, pushing their implied yields lower.               \par \par \uc2 "We interpret this as a direct warning that the RBNZ may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by selling \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars," said Westpac chief economist Dominick Stephens.               \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ had warned in May it might sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if conditions were opportune.               \par \par \uc2 Its latest statement said the economy was expected to grow 3.7 percent this year, but a sharp fall in some commodity prices would feed through to primary sector incomes.               \par \par \uc2 The RBNZ said inflation was moderate, house price growth has slowed and wages were subdued, but spare capacity was being used up, driven by the earthquake reconstruction in Canterbury and strong migration gains.               \par \par \uc2 Separately data showed the economy notching another solid trade surplus in June, with the annual surplus within sight of last month's near 20-year high.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-7-24T04:50:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140723ea7n00dan\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
