\par\par \b \uc2 Australia dollar shines after upbeat data, NZ dollar struggles\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2014\par 05:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie up 0.5 pct vs USD, kiwi & GBP\par \par \uc2 * China trade numbers, upbeat Australia jobs growth boost AUD\par \par \uc2 * NZD hurt by RBNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat\par \par \uc2 By Cecile Lefort and Naomi Tajitsu\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, May 8 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar received a fillip on Thursday from upbeat data in Australia and China, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar still nursed losses following a threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Aussie rose half a cent to $0.9374, though it faces tough resistance at $0.9400, the 76.4 percent retracement of the April-May fall. It was pulling closer to this year's peak of $0.9461, having gained 5 percent since Jan. 1.\par \par \uc2 The move higher came after China's trade numbers beat expectations, with exports rising 0.9 percent on the year in April. Imports from Australia grew almost 18 percent in April from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie had already received a leg up on a solid labour report at home. Australian jobs growth outpaced expectations for a third month running in April and helped keep the jobless rate from rising.\par \par \uc2 The outcome will be welcomed by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 , which again kept rates on hold this week in expectation that past cuts were working to reenergise the economy.\par \par \uc2 The futures market <0#YIB:> dipped a touch as it had already priced out any chance of another easing, while swap markets slightly increased the chance that the next move in rates would be up, albeit not until 2015.\par \par \uc2 "The market is probably a little too complacent on how long they expect the cash rate to remain unchanged. They don't have a full hike priced until September of next year," said \uc2 Su-Lin Ong\uc2 , an economist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Aussie rallied across the board and showed the largest increase against its kiwi neighbour. It jumped 0.5 percent to NZ$1.0821, well above a one-month low of NZ$1.0642 touched on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Against the U.S. dollar, the New Zealand dollar was stuck at $0.8654. Investors dumped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday when the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  warned that a historically strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could slow the pace of future interest rate rises.\par \par \uc2 In a speech, RBNZ Graeme Wheeler had also put the possibility of kiwi-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the table if economic fundamentals no longer justify \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. His comments pushed the kiwi down 0.9 percent, its biggest daily fall since late January.\par \par \uc2 Broad selling knocked the kiwi down to its lowest in roughly a week against the euro. As a result, the kiwi fell to 80.08 versus a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, retreating from 81.00 on Wednesday, when it matched a post-float high.\par \par \uc2 Most economists still expect the RBNZ to raise rates to 3.25 percent in June, but analysts said that risks were growing that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may then pause its tightening cycle if the kiwi stays strong.\par \par \uc2 "It's nice to see some domestic factors emerging in the kiwi pricing, but the ultimate driver is going to be the U.S. dollar," ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sam Tuck said.\par \par \uc2 "We'd expect to see decent demand below $0.8600."\par \par \uc2 New Zealand government bonds rose, pushing yields 3 basis points lower at the long end of the curve.\par \par \uc2 Australian government bond futures retreated from multimonth highs, with the three-year bond contract down 3 ticks at 97.120. The 10-year contract was steady at 95.880, having touched a seven-month high of 96.190.   (Editing by \uc2 Chris Gallagher\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-11T03:38:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140508ea580063h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
