\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1 -NZ jobless holds at 5-yr low, cenbank seen hiking again in June\b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2014\par 02:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * NZ jobless rate flat at 6 pct in Q4\par \par \uc2 * Jobless rate stays at lowest since Q2 2009\par \par \uc2 * Data bolsters expectations for RBNZ rate rise\par \par \uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu\par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, May 7 (Reuters) - New Zealand's jobless rate remained at its lowest in five years in the first quarter and a record number were in the workforce, maintaining expectations for another interest rate increase next month.\par \par \uc2 The jobless rate was unchanged at 6.0 percent in the three months to March, \uc2 Statistics New Zealand\uc2  said on Wednesday. This was higher than economist forecasts for a 5.9 percent print, but the rate stayed at its lowest since the June 2009 quarter and points to a growing labour market as the economy strengthens.\par \par \uc2 Employment growth was driven by the jobs in the construction industry, helping to boost the labour participation rate to 69.3 percent, the highest on data going back to 1986. The number of people employed rose by a seasonally adjusted 22,000.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar slipped to $0.8693 after the data from around $0.8723 beforehand, as investors sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the slightly higher-than-expected jobless rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had earlier tumbled in early trade after Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor warned the bank may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wheeler said if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed high in the face of worsening fundamentals, such as continuing weakening in export prices, "it would become more opportune for the Reserve Bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell NZ dollars."\par \par \uc2 Wheeler also said a high \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would contain tradable inflation pressures and would be a factor in its rate-raising decisions.\par \par \uc2 Still, Tuesday jobs report pointed to ongoing growth in the labour market, suggesting the RBNZ will raise official interest rates by 25 basis points to 3.25 percent at its next review in June, following two similar hikes in March and April.\par \par \uc2 "This is a pretty strong report and this is how the RBNZ will see it. For interest rates, it doesn't change the move to 4.5 percent by next year," said Annette Beacher, head of Asia-Pacific research at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 "I see the RBNZ tightening in June, then rest for a couple of months."\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation rose 1.5 percent in the first quarter, staying near the mid-point of the RBNZ's 1 percent-3 percent target range and keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its monetary tightening track after it began raising rates in March.\par \par \uc2 More New Zealanders are finding jobs as the country's $180 billion economy continues to grow at a solid pace on the back of earthquake reconstruction projects in the Canterbury region, a booming housing market and increasing dairy exports.\par \par \uc2 Employment gains were led by job opportunities in construction in the Canterbury region, along with retail and other services industries in Auckland, the country's largest city.\par \par \uc2 Private sector wages edged up 0.3 percent in the latest quarter, maintaining a rising trend albeit at a slower pace than economist forecasts for a 0.5 percent rise.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said that a further slowing in wage growth as strong labour demand is met by increasing participation may quell overall inflation pressures, taking pressure off the RBNZ to raise rates down the line.\par \par \uc2 "Rising cyclical potential growth should ... go some way to containing inflationary pressure in forthcoming quarters," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  analysts said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "And the fact that wages grew a very benign 0.3 percent in Q1 underscores the point that measures of slack across the New Zealand are well behaved, and argue against the magnitude of tightening currently implicit in RBNZ forecasts."\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of 17 economists shows that all but one expect the RBNZ to raise rates to 3.25 percent in June, with most economists anticipating rates to rise to 3.75 percent by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Naomi Tajitsu; Editing by Shri Navaratnam)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-10T00:53:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140507ea57001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
