\par\par \b \uc2 Kiwi Tumbles as RBNZ Refers to Possible  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2014\par 00:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 18:22 EDT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar slips as RBNZ Governor Graeme Wheeler attempts to jawbone the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower in a speech to dairy farmers. Wheeler says the Kiwi is overvalued and if the "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains high in the face of worsening fundamentals, such as a continued weakening in export prices, it would become more opportune for the Reserve Bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell NZ dollars." He also underscored that the high dollar--along with new economic data--will be a factor in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assessment of the extent and speed with which the official cash rate needs to be raised. NZD/USD at 0.8717, from 0.8742 before the speech. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2204 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian markets may come under pressure after the major U.S. indexes ended lower amid geopolitical tensions that caused some risk aversion, says James Smalley, broker at Hamilton Hindin Greene in New Zealand. "This seems to be affecting growth sensitive stocks." He says he doesn't expect an impact from news Chinese Internet giant \uc2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\uc2 . has officially filed plans to offer its shares in the U.S., in what could be one of the largest stock listings in history. The filing came after U.S. markets closed. Some focus may fall on Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), which is making a presentation to analysts in Australia Wednesday. New Zealand's NZX-50 ended down 0.5%, or 25 points, at 5174.90 Tuesday. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;Twitter: @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 17:44 EDT - Despite a slowing economy, China remained the No. 1 destination for foreign-direct investment in 2013, the latest OECD estimates show. China's total came to $258.2B, up 2%. The US attracted $193.4B, up 16%, to rank No. 2. China gobbled up about 19% of the global total, the US 14%, Brazil 4.6%, Canada 4.5%, Russia 4% and Australia 3.7%. (bob.hagerty@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:21 EDT - BMO Capital Markets says Toronto home sales were a modest 1.8% above year-ago levels in April, leaving the level of activity almost right on the 10-year average. But prices are pushing higher at a brisk pace, with the average transaction price now up more than 10% year-over-year. BMO cays the Toronto Real Estate Board is citing a lack of active listings, which were down more than 8.4% yoy in the month. "Note the disparity in average price growth between the tight single detached market (+11.3% y/y) and more amply-supplied condos (+2.9% y/y)," firm adds. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 17:18 EDT - There are a number of domestic events scheduled for the session that may move NZD/USD and AUD/USD, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Imre Speizer, who sees an upside bias for the AUD/USD but a possible correction lower for the NZD/USD. In New Zealand, Speizer says RBNZ Governor Wheeler is speaking in Hamilton, and then 1Q labor data is due. "The market is likely to focus on the unemployment rate, expecting an improvement to 5.8%, but wage cost data will also be watched." He says AUD focus is on Australia retail sales. NZD/USD last at 0.8742, which Speizer sees correcting towards 0.8680 before pushing to "fresh highs." AUD/USD last at 0.9350, targeting 0.9380. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 17:06 EDT - NZD/USD higher as USD positions were liquidated across the board, says ANZ. Firm adds there will likely be little respite from a strengthening New Zealand dollar with the release of 1Q employment data expected to be broadly supportive. "We expect today's employment data to confirm a further pick-up in the labor market with employment growth forecast to rise 3.4% on year--an 8-year high--and the unemployment rate to fall to a 5-year low of 5.7% in 1Q." NZD/USD at 0.8743, from 0.8695 late last session. ANZ expects the pair to trade in a short-term range of 0.8680 to 0.8840. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;Twitter: @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:52 EDT - Morgan Stanley Chairman and CEO \uc2 James Gorman\uc2  has been tough on his Wall Street peers before, saying some were overpaid and confused brains with a bull market before the 2008 financial crisis. But that doesn't mean they broke the law, he says Tuesday afternoon at a conference in New York. Gorman defends law-enforcement officials for failing to put more people in jail for their actions during the financial crisis. "Bad judgment, incompetence, negligence...might be socially unacceptable, but they're not criminal offenses," Gorman says. "We have laws on the books for a reason." (justin.baer@wsj.com, @JustinBaer)\par \par \uc2 16:48 EDT - Early stage tech startups have been insulated from the capital flight that has hit public tech companies like Twitter (TWTR) and forced startups like Box Inc. to delay their IPOs, according to Jonathan Lehr, venture director at Work-Bench, a growth accelerator for business technology. That could change, though, he warns. "When a company like Box delays its IPO, it is driven more by market conditions," Lehr says. "If things continue like this, the impact will trickle down to Series C deals. I don't expect an impact any time soon on the Series A and B deals," but "there could be a potential impact on early stage companies down the road." (steven.rosenbush@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:36 EDT - Natural gas producers will have a little more work to do this coming winter than forecasters expected. The EIA says Tuesday that the gas industry is likely to produce 0.5B cubic feet a day more in November and December than originally thought. It'll have to, too. The ramp-up is to overcome slower-than-expected production growth this April that's come at a time when demand is still strong. Government forecasters now expect consumption to average 72.3B cubic feet a day for 2014, and for production to keep up a record pace through the winter in order to match it. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:30 EDT - The Fed takes away the punch bowl and the economy nears contraction in 1Q. Coincidence? Actually, yes, says Paul Dales, senior US economist at Capital Economics. The Fed's move in December to start reducing the amount of bonds it purchases monthly is not the cause of the winter's economic weakness, he says. Recent data shows job growth and consumer spending accelerating this spring even as the Fed continues to pull back. "The big fear was that tapering the asset purchases would send Treasury yields soaring and stock markets plummeting," Dales said. "Of course, this is exactly the reverse of what has actually happened." So what was to blame? The weather. (eric.morath@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:16 EDT - \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's top executive says the US has emerged from the worst financial crisis in decades to become what may be the world's strongest economy. \uc2 James Gorman\uc2 , the bank's chairman and CEO, said during a conference in New York that the pace of the recovery hasn't been as slow as some critics note. After all, he says, it took more than a decade following the Great Depression to bring the nation's jobless rate below 10%. This time around, the US is recovering at a much faster pace, he said. "This economy may be the strongest economy in the world right now," Gorman says. (justin.baer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:58 EDT - Canada's aging population is a key issue and warrants more study. Yet, after Statistics Canada conducted a one-off survey of older workers for a government department--which foot the bill--several years ago, it didn't look into continuing the program using internal funds. Canada's Auditor General found that users of the data extended beyond the paying department and recommended Statistics Canada assess whether to continue such work even after external funding ends, "to ensure that priority data needs are met." The agency agrees and is directing managers to review newly discontinued programs, with an eye on possibly funding those considered "of strategic importance for the national statistical systems" from its own budget. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 06, 2014 18:22 ET (22:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140506ea56004pq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
