\par\par \b \uc2 Kiwi Tumbles as RBNZ Refers to Possible  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2014\par 00:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 18:22 EDT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar slips as RBNZ Governor Graeme Wheeler attempts to jawbone the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower in a speech to dairy farmers. Wheeler says the Kiwi is overvalued and if the "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains high in the face of worsening fundamentals, such as a continued weakening in export prices, it would become more opportune for the Reserve Bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell NZ dollars." He also underscored that the high dollar--along with new economic data--will be a factor in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assessment of the extent and speed with which the official cash rate needs to be raised. NZD/USD at 0.8717, from 0.8742 before the speech. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 18:13 EDT - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  has upgraded Santos (STO.AU) to Overweight from Neutral, while raising its price target by 5.2% to A$14.41. "Following a marked 7-month period of underperformance relative to peers Woodside (WPL.AU), Oil Search and Origin (ORG.AU), we now believe the risk/reward equation again favors an Overweight position in Santos," the broker says. "With PNG LNG due to commence LNG sales around mid-year, and the rationale behind purchasing 3rd party gas for the GLNG project becoming clearer, we think there is a strong case for the 17%-21% free cash flow yield we forecast for the latter part of this decade to compress." STO last A$13.94. (david.rogers@wsj.com; @DRWSJ)\par \par \uc2 17:56 EDT - \uc2 Electronic Arts (EA) \uc2 is pretty pleased with "Titanfall." The sci-fi shooting game, made by the original creators of "Call of Duty," was released in March to high anticipation. But EA stayed silent about sales, other than when market researcher NPD said it was the top selling game in March. Now, we know by how much: It sold 925k copies between console and PC, according to NPD. Good results, EA says. (ian.sherr@wsj.com; @iansherr)\par \par \uc2 17:54 EDT - Audacious is a good word for the 5-year business plant Fiat Chrysler CEO \uc2 Sergio Marchionne\uc2  has rolled out, especially considering Chrysler was bankrupt five years ago. Marchionne made bold forecasts five years ago and has hit many of his targets, but he had tailwinds with the US coming out of recession. The landscape is tougher now. The US market will grow by about 1M units to 17M cars and trucks, so much of Fiat Chrysler's growth in the US will have to come by taking share from other auto makers. Fiat's plan forecasts US market share of 15.8% by 2018--a 4.4 point increase from 11.4% in 2013. It's a tall task, considering domestic rivals GM and Ford (F) are more competitive. (neal.boudette@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:44 EDT - Despite a slowing economy, China remained the No. 1 destination for foreign-direct investment in 2013, the latest OECD estimates show. China's total came to $258.2B, up 2%. The US attracted $193.4B, up 16%, to rank No. 2. China gobbled up about 19% of the global total, the US 14%, Brazil 4.6%, Canada 4.5%, Russia 4% and Australia 3.7%. (bob.hagerty@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:39 EDT - Alibaba's initial SEC filing makes clear something that \uc2 Yahoo\uc2  (YHOO) discovered in 2011: The Chinese company's corporate structure gives shareholders something less-than-full ownership of the good stuff. The document describes how licenses to operate its various websites are held by structures called variable interest entities "generally majority-owned by Jack Ma" to comply with Chinese law. The arrangement, the filing notes, "may not be as effective in providing \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  control as direct ownership." The structure came into play in 2011, when Alibaba made its e-payment arm Alipay a separate business, triggering a dispute with shareholder \uc2 Yahoo\uc2 . (carlos.tejada@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:34 EDT - All day long, \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2  has been spelling out ambitious plans -- eight models for Alfa Romeo, six for Maserati, international expansion for Jeep, growth in auto sales everywhere. Now it's spelling out what it thinks what all that will do for its financials: For one, a massive increase in group revenue, a 50% leap, from 87 billion euros in 2013 to 93 billion in 2014, to 132 billion in 2018. FCA sees a drop in net income this year - to 600 million to 800 million euros from 2013's 900 million. But then it expects the bottom line to grow - to a range of 4.7 billion to 5.5 billion euros in 2018. (neal.boudette@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:34 EDT - Alibaba values itself at roughly $116B as of April, according to its financial disclosures published Tuesday. The figure is based on the $50-per-share price at which the company granted stock-based compensation last month and some 2.3B outstanding shares. Analysts have projected that when the company stages its initial public offering, expected later this summer, it will be valued between $150B and $250B. (rolfe.winkler@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:29 EDT - First Solar's (FSLR) stock jumps nearly 4% to about $70 after the company reports 1Q earnings nearly doubled from a year ago, to $112M, or $1.10 a share. Not bad for a company that transformed itself from one of the world's largest solar-panel suppliers to a major North American solar-farm developer in just two years. The Tempe, Ariz.-based solar company booked net sales of $950.2M in 1Q, up 26% from a year earlier, helped by the sale of its 139-megawatt Campo Verde solar farm in California to \uc2 Southern Co\uc2 . (SO) and Turner Renewable Energy. (cassandra.sweet@wsj.com; @cassandrasweet)\par \par \uc2 17:28 EDT - \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2 ' all-day presentation boils down to this: Do you believe CEO \uc2 Sergio Marchionne\uc2  can deliver by 2018 a nearly three-fold increase in EBITDA, a roughly 40% increase in revenues, and a 90% reduction in net debt? Mr. Marchionne and his team remind investors that they confounded doubters with the results they achieved during the past five years. But the soon-to-be merged FCA had a tailwind from the strong recovery in U.S. auto demand after the 2008-2009 collapse. The road could be tougher in the U.S. going forward as the market levels off. (joseph.white@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:21 EDT - BMO Capital Markets says Toronto home sales were a modest 1.8% above year-ago levels in April, leaving the level of activity almost right on the 10-year average. But prices are pushing higher at a brisk pace, with the average transaction price now up more than 10% year-over-year. BMO cays the Toronto Real Estate Board is citing a lack of active listings, which were down more than 8.4% yoy in the month. "Note the disparity in average price growth between the tight single detached market (+11.3% y/y) and more amply-supplied condos (+2.9% y/y)," firm adds. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 17:19 EDT - Wall Street's patience with \uc2 Aegerion Pharmaceuticals\uc2  (AEGR) is running thin, with the drug company's shares down 16% to $37 after-hours. AEGR reports a 1Q sales miss and lowers its full-year guidance, blaming the poor outlook on an anti-corruption investigation in Brazil, the second largest market for AEGR's cholesterol-lowering drug outside the U.S. Through the close, AEGR shares had already slipped 37% year-to-date. AEGR CEO \uc2 Marc Beer\uc2  says the company's troubles are "manageable," but investors will likely want to see proof in quarters to come before the stock rebounds meaningfully. (joseph.walker@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 06, 2014 18:22 ET (22:22 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140506ea56004n9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
