\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China to allow direct yuan, NZ dollar trades\b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 22:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment, details)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING/WELLINGTON, March 19 (Reuters) - China has allowed direct domestic trading of the yuan against the New Zealand dollar to encourage such trading as it internationalises the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The move, announced on Tuesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a visit by New Zealand Prime Minister \uc2 John Key\uc2  to Beijing, comes after China doubled the yuan's trading band over the weekend in a milestone step that gives investors more freedom to set the value of the tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China is New Zealand's largest export destination and a major buyer of dairy products produced in the South Pacific nation. The move was seen as promoting trade between the two countries, which rose 25.2 percent to NZ$18.2 billion ($15.71 billion) in 2013, and increase capital flows.\par \par \uc2 "Today's announcement will further reduce the costs of trade and investment transactions between New Zealand and China, increasing the ease of doing business for the benefit of businesspeople in both countries," New Zealand Prime Minister \uc2 John Key\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 As part of China's sweeping plans to overhaul its maturing economy and let market forces drive a host of industries, the government wants to gradually relax its hold over the yuan and turn it into a global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that one day rivals the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The government's wish to promote international use of the yuan is partly driven by its concern that China is too vulnerable to the fluctuating value of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 China is home to the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, worth $3.82 trillion at the end of last year. About a third is invested in \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 To promote international use of the yuan, China has signed a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with foreign governments in order to increase the overseas circulation of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Beijing has also allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to directly trade a string of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs within China to lower transaction costs. The New Zealand dollar is the 10th foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that can be directly traded against the yuan in China.\par \par \uc2 And some data suggests China is making headway in its efforts to raise the yuan's profile.\par \par \uc2 Global transaction services organisation SWIFT said in December that the yuan overtook the euro in October to become the second-most used \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade finance.\par \par \uc2 But despite rising use of the yuan overseas and the apparent greater freedom that investors have in trading it, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still keeps the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a tight leash with traders reporting regular \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. ($1 = 1.1587 \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dollars) (Reporting by Koh Gui Qing in Beijing, additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu in Wellington; Editing by Robert Birsel and Keiron Henderson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-18T22:07:26.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140318ea3i00el3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580