\par\par \b \uc2 Australian dollar on the ropes after RBA; kiwi off recent lows\b0\par\par\uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2013\par 03:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * AUD near 3-mth low vs USD and euro, 3-yr low vs GBP\par \par \uc2 * Aussie hit by RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment, upbeat US data\par \par \uc2 * AUD and NZD down 1.3 pct vs USD since Monday\par \par \uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu and Cecile Lefort\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar licked its wounds on Friday after the head of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said he was open to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar bounced off lows as investors squared up short positions.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie skidded to a near three-month low of $0.9198 in offshore trade before edging back up to $0.9244.\par \par \uc2 It was also hard hit against the euro and the pound. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to a near three-month peak of A$1.4621 , bringing in sight the August high of A$1.5029. The pound surged to a three-year peak overnight with charts pointing to further upside. It was last at A$1.7520.\par \par \uc2 Much of the pressure for the Aussie came after Reserve Bank of Australia Governor \uc2 Glenn Stevens\uc2  stepped up his rhetoric against the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he has long argued was overvalued when judged against economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 While Stevens made clear that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not without risks, markets were in the mood to sell the Aussie especially after a closely watched report showed China's factory sector grew at a slower pace in November.\par \par \uc2 Not helping was encouraging U.S. data that underpinned the U.S. dollar, as well as expectations the Federal Reserve will have to start tapering its asset buying at some point, whether December or March.\par \par \uc2 All that left the Aussie nursing hefty losses for the week. It was down 1.3 percent against the U.S. dollar since Monday and a clear break below $0.9200, the 61.8 percent of the August-October rally, would open the way to a test of a three-year low of $0.8848.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the New Zealand dollar managed to pop up to a session high of $0.8245 following tame comments by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official about kiwi strength.\par \par \uc2 Investors had expected a stronger complaint by Reserve Bank of New Zealand Assistant Governor \uc2 John McDermott\uc2 , who said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overvalued and that the RBNZ would like to see it lower.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi was broadly stronger, clocking an intraday high around 83.40 yen. It also shone against the Aussie , which slid to around NZ$1.1194, closing in on a two-month low around NZ$1.1192 hit earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that the RBNZ will raise rates from a record-low 2.5 percent early next year also helped the kiwi .\par \par \uc2 Market participants said McDermott's comments were seen as part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's usual jaw-boning against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, in contrast with the RBA's more aggressive effort to talk down the Aussie.\par \par \uc2 "There was no smoking gun in the speech. All it said was that the kiwi is overvalued, there was nothing new in it at all," said Tim Kelleher, head of institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at ASB.\par \par \uc2 Traders had bought back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it fell to a one-week low of $0.8175 on Thursday, tumbling in sympathy with losses in the Aussie. Yet, the kiwi was still down 1.3 percent since Monday.\par \par \uc2 Kelleher said that despite its slide the previous day, support had held at the kiwi's 200-day moving average of $0.8173, which also prompted demand. But he added that offers seen above $0.8250 would likely limit further gains for now.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand government bonds inched lower, nudging yields 1.5 basis points higher across the curve. Bonds remained on the back foot following relatively weak demand at an offer of inflation-linked bonds on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Australian government bond futures were a touch softer with the long-end of the curve at 20-month lows. The three-year bond contract eased 1 tick to 96.820, while the 10-year contract fell as deep as 95.700, a level unseen since March 2012. A break below 95.600 would test the lowest in nearly three years. (Editing by \uc2 Chris Gallagher\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | AUF | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ND | ABX | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020131125e9bm00028\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
