\par\par \b \uc2 NZD/USD Gets Lift On RBNZ Saying  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Would Have No Sustained Impact -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2013\par 00:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD got a lift after RBNZ Assistant Governor John McDermott said it was overvalued but that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market would have little impact. "Whilst the bulk of his speech was the usual rhetoric about the overvalued Kiwi, it looks like the market has dismissed it off hand as jawboning and instead focused on his comments on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the futility of it, says Chris Hunter, corporate dealing manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Western Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  Business Solutions. The pair is at 0.8225 versus 0.8204 prior to McDermott's speech in which he noted daily foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 New Zealand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollars "swamp any practical \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capacity," as they average around NZ$100 billion a day with more than 80% of transactions taking place offshore. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to trade with a bullish bias near-term after hitting a three-week high of $95.63/bbl Thursday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is positive-biased as the MACD indicator is bullish, the slow stochastic measure is rising from the oversold level, while the five-day moving average looks poised to stage a bullish crossover against the 15-day moving average. A rise above the $95.63 resistance would expose the upside to $96.65 (Nov. 1 high), then to $97.03 (Oct. 31 high), $97.82 (Oct. 30 high) and $98.57 (Oct. 29 high, near the 200-day moving average). But a drop below $93.47 (Thursday's low) would temper the positive near-term view, targeting $93.25 (Wednesday's low), then $92.43 (Tuesday's reaction low), $91.26 (June 3 reaction low), $90.11 (May 1 reaction low) and the psychological $90.00 line. January crude is down 19 cents at $95.25 on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at six foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 3.4 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders. The eighth consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for the market at the open, although the yen value basis of the figures is unknown. Buy orders total 14.7 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 11.3 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 18:14 EST - For the first time since mid-2006, 10-year US Treasurys yield 104 bps more than comparable German bunds. This spread has been expanding sharply since mid-October as investors got past fears about the \uc2 US government\uc2  shutdown and turned to the two central banks on opposite sides the Atlantic looking poised to take diverging policy paths. "Tightening on its way in the US while Europe considers new unconventional easing measures," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. Firm is shorting 10-year Treasurys versus 10-year bunds, targeting a 110 bps spread. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may trade in a 100.60-101.60 range with bias on the upside, says Daisaku Ueno, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . The dollar jumped to its highest level in four months against the yen Thursday, after strong employment data bolstered the view that the Fed will begin a rollback of its monthly bond-purchasing program soon. "After the pair's surge, I think we can expect the Nikkei to start strong, which could boost risk appetite and lead to further yen-selling," he says. "And if the pair manages to break through the July 8 high of 101.54, it could trigger stop-loss orders and climb higher," he adds. However, he says position adjustments were bound to kick in at some point, which could limit the pair's upside. He tips the EUR/USD at 1.3425-1.3550. The USD/JPY is at 101.16 and the EUR/USD is at 1.3476. (alexander.martin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2229 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian equities are likely to rebound from declines the last four days, chasing gains on Wall Street overnight. IG predicts the S&P/ASX 200 will climb 0.6% early, and expects gold stocks to again be in focus following further weakness in the bullion price. The iron ore price was mildly lower, but IG notes \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) is headed for a rise after its U.S. ADR ended up almost 0.2%. Investors continue to digest comments late Thursday from the head of \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who said he was "open-minded" on whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try and push the Australian dollar lower, comments that could weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, which would benefit exporters. \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2 's (RIO.AU) chairman is scheduled to speak Friday at an Australian-British Chamber of Commerce lunch, and his comments will come on the heels of fairly optimistic words from the chairman of BHP on demand from China and the recovery in the U.S. economy. The ASX 200 ended Thursday at 5288.3. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:14 EST - Two data releases loom large for the Canadian dollar Friday morning: October CPI and September retail sales. But \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says it doesn't expect them to provide much support for the struggling CAD. Seeing just a 0.1% on-month rise in sales and inflation remaining subdued, BNP expects to see USD/CAD trend higher. USD/CAD at 1.0519 after notching a session high of 1.0529, from Wednesday's 1.0449 close. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 16:59 EST - For all the cited risks that make an IOER cut improbable, Pantheon Macroeconomics' Ian Shepherdson says there are benefits to be considered. He says cutting the interest paid on excess reserves to, say, 0.05% from the current 0.25%, would reduce the amount banks earn through this channel from $7.25B to $1.5B next year, assuming tapering starts in June. While it's not realistic that banks will start dishing out loans immediately to make up for that lost income, Shepherdson says the point is that the shortfall is big enough that it'll prompt banks to "work hard to increase lending." (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 15:18 EST - Ditch those notions of a potential Fed tapering in December. "The Fed is in no hurry," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  writes. "It is worth noting that policy decisions are often driven by the Committee leadership, and there is a disconnect in the tone of recent communication from the Chairman and Vice Chair and the tone of the October minutes," firm says. "Investors are now focused on whether a strong November payroll report could lead the Fed to taper in December. While the employment leg of Bernanke's three part test is flashing green, neither growth nor inflation is showing convincing signs of acceleration, and we see the odds of the Fed tapering into thin December markets as quite low." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 14:19 EST - Unsurprisingly, the hot topic of the ECB possibly using a negative deposit rate as an easing tool came up a number of times during Q&A for executive board member Joerg Asmussen at an event in New York. He says he'd be "very, very cautious" in using the instrument, but that he would not "categorically exclude it" as an option. One worry he has is that cutting the rate could signal that the ECB is more concerned about the economy than it really is. When asked again about the tool, Asmussen said a negative rate has its advantages and disadvantages, having studied its use in Demark, but added that "the ECB is not Denmark." (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1919 GMT [Dow Jones] A speech by RBA Governor Glenn Stevens overnight "continued the RBA's series of jawboning assaults on the AUD," says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones. While Stevens suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower the AUD was likely to be ineffective and costly at the present given the easy monetary policies in the U.S., Europe and Japan, "the speech also served up a thinly veiled warning to markets that the RBA's reticence to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the recent past does not mean it will eschew such action in the future," Jones says. Stevens said "\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can, judiciously used in the right circumstances, be effective and useful." Jones says the RBNZ may make similar comments in a speech this morning. The AUD/USD is at 0.9219 early in New Zealand versus 0.9296 late Thursday in Australia. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1905 GMT [Dow Jones] A RBNZ speech about influences on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may drive the NZD/USD on the day and "should an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  speech be delivered, it could give us a reason to break to the next support level at 0.8100-0.8120, says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . The pair is at 0.8192 early in New Zealand. Assistant Governor John McDermott will speak about influences on the exchange rate at 11:30 local time (2230 GMT). \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  notes, however, net migration data--due at 10:45 local time--will continue to support the NZD. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 21, 2013 18:58 ET (23:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131121e9bl0059k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
