\par\par \b \uc2 Australia & NZ dlrs rally on rousing jobs news\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2013\par 03:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Surge in jobs data triggers A$ & NZ$ short-covering\par \par \uc2 * Aussie & kiwi up 0.5 pct on the day vs USD\par \par \uc2 * Markets pare back chance of June rate cut in Australia\par \par \uc2 By Gyles Beckford and Cecile Lefort\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, May 9 (Reuters) - The Australian and New Zealand dollars bounced on Thursday following stellar labour reports in both countries, prompting markets to pare back the risk of a June rate cut in Australia while reinforcing the outlook for steady to higher NZ rates.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar gained 0.6 percent on the day to $1.0240. Its recovery from a two-month low of $1.0155 hit twice this week caught many speculators short and forced them to buy back the Aussie.\par \par \uc2 The local dollar jumped as high as $1.0255 where it met heavy resistance. A break there would open the way to $1.0318, the 38.2 percent retracement of the April-May fall.\par \par \uc2 Australian employment surged by 50,100 in April to more than make up for a drop the previous month and nudge the jobless rate to 5.5 percent, a resoundingly upbeat report that contrasted with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to cut interest rates on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "While this survey is notoriously volatile, it does give us more confidence that the RBA (\uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 ) will stand pat, at least for now, after this week's cut," said Alvin Pontoh, a strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Interbank futures <0#YIB:> duly eased as the market tinkered with expectations of the timing of another cut. A move by July now looks more like a 50-50 bet than the sure thing priced in before the jobs news.\par \par \uc2 Still, the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one reason the RBA eased to a record low of 2.75 percent on Tuesday, so further gains would likely add to the case for another move in time.\par \par \uc2 "We suspect the Bank's easing bias isn't going anywhere while the AUD trades around $1.02-$1.05, as part of the RBA's apparent management of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Pontoh.\par \par \uc2 Yields on three-year government cash bonds bounced off six-month lows to last trade at 2.579 percent, from 2.481 percent.\par \par \uc2 Australian government bond futures followed suit with the three-year contract down 0.060 points to 97.440, some way off 97.530 set this week, the highest since October.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year contract eased 0.045 points to 96.830, with the yield curve flattening.\par \par \uc2 RBNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  EFFORTS ERASED\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was propelled about 0.6 percent higher on the day to $0.8459.\par \par \uc2 It got its first lift of around half a cent after data showed New Zealand added a record 38,000 jobs in the first quarter, pushing the jobless rate down to a three-year low of 6.2 percent..\par \par \uc2 The rally was extended after Australia's jobs data, taking the kiwi as high as $0.8481. That recouped all the losses caused by Wednesday's news the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 New Zealand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBNZ) had been quietly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The markets liked it (the data), they were probably a bit short given the comments from the \uc2 Reserve Bank\uc2  (of NZ) yesterday, and it's back to where it was before then," said ASB Bank head of institutional sales Tim Kelleher.\par \par \uc2 "Locally it's been a bit of risk on, and following on from the positive equities in New York ... it will be a struggle to make Aussie and kiwi come off tonight."\par \par \uc2 Near term resistance was seen just below $0.8500, at the 20 day moving average, and above that Wednesday's high of $0.84524, with support at $0.84253.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the often volatile labour data was regarded with some suspicion and it was not considered convincing enough to alter the RBNZ's outlook for steady rates this year.\par \par \uc2 "This largely reflects the current backdrop of muted inflation out turns and expectations, together with the elevated level of the NZ dollar continuing to dampen down tradable inflation," said First NZ Capital's director of economics, Chris Green.\par \par \uc2 The RBNZ's concerns about a frothy house market would hardly have been assuaged by data showing national house prices rose 7.1 percent in the year to April to a record high.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand government bond yields rose as much as 7 basis points at the long end of the curve.\par \par \uc2 (Australia and New Zealand bureaux)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-NEWZEALAND/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 3) | AUF | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ND | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130509e95900021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
